WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:09.180
- It is both a great pleasure
and a great privilege to

00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:12.720
introduce our guest speaker
today, Mr. Rizvi Hassan,

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:15.856
who reaches us from Dhaka, Bangladesh.

00:00:15.856 --> 00:00:18.470
Rizvi Hassan has completed many projects

00:00:18.470 --> 00:00:20.295
for the Bangladeshis and,

00:00:20.295 --> 00:00:23.100
for the Rohingya refugees in the world's

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:26.520
largest refugee camp in Kutupalong.

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:30.420
Within five years of completing
his bachelor of architecture

00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:34.007
at the Bangladesh University
of Engineering and Technology,

00:00:34.007 --> 00:00:38.295
Rizvi Hassan received
numerous awards and accolades.

00:00:38.295 --> 00:00:41.862
In 2020, his Beyond Survival safe space

00:00:41.862 --> 00:00:44.552
in Teknaf refugee camp was

00:00:44.552 --> 00:00:49.552
singled out by The Guardian as
one of the world's top 10 new

00:00:49.950 --> 00:00:51.810
architectural projects.

00:00:51.810 --> 00:00:53.730
Two years later in 2022,

00:00:53.730 --> 00:00:55.929
he received the distinguished
and much coveted

00:00:55.929 --> 00:00:59.465
Aga Khan Award for Architecture.

00:00:59.465 --> 00:01:01.404
He has been named by the UN

00:01:01.404 --> 00:01:05.850
a real life hero and acknowledged by OCHA,

00:01:05.850 --> 00:01:10.062
the United Nations Office
for Humanitarian Affairs

00:01:10.062 --> 00:01:13.560
for his work with the Rohingyas.

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:18.330
Rizvi Hassan has innovated
on so many different fronts,

00:01:18.330 --> 00:01:21.472
broken so many stereotypes
and barriers that it is

00:01:21.472 --> 00:01:24.630
impossible to do justice to his creativity

00:01:24.630 --> 00:01:26.763
in the space of a few words.

00:01:26.763 --> 00:01:30.810
His architecture is a bold
and visionary critique of the

00:01:30.810 --> 00:01:35.100
soulless shelters and buildings
being done all over the

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:38.553
world for impoverished
population and for refugees.

00:01:39.570 --> 00:01:43.890
And part of his success comes
from his profound engagement

00:01:43.890 --> 00:01:45.900
with the refugees themselves.

00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:48.120
Before embarking on any project,

00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:52.440
Rizvi Hassan holds workshops
with the Rohingya refugees,

00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:53.730
including the children,

00:01:53.730 --> 00:01:56.640
trying to find out their
ideas and their suggestions.

00:01:56.640 --> 00:01:58.260
This of course takes time, because

00:01:58.260 --> 00:02:01.860
it requires earning
their trust beforehand.

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:05.220
But as a result of these workshops,

00:02:05.220 --> 00:02:07.890
he identifies craftsmen, craftswomen,

00:02:07.890 --> 00:02:11.199
artists, carpenters, weavers, teachers,

00:02:11.199 --> 00:02:16.199
and is eager to hear their
ideas, and then they collaborate

00:02:16.535 --> 00:02:19.221
with him so that the
final project reflects

00:02:19.221 --> 00:02:21.753
what they have suggested to him.

00:02:22.810 --> 00:02:25.842
The Rohingyas who fled genocide in Myanmar

00:02:25.842 --> 00:02:28.140
are a stateless minority.

00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:29.610
They have absolutely nothing.

00:02:29.610 --> 00:02:33.540
They possess nothing
but intangible heritage.

00:02:33.540 --> 00:02:38.130
And these projects,
Rizvi's projects with them,

00:02:38.130 --> 00:02:42.764
reflect and give body, shape,
and form to this heritage.

00:02:42.764 --> 00:02:46.811
Rizvi Hassan has designed a
series of centers in different

00:02:46.811 --> 00:02:51.210
Rohingya camps, all
with different projects,

00:02:51.210 --> 00:02:52.920
a cultural memory center that

00:02:52.920 --> 00:02:56.767
addresses issues of memory and identity,

00:02:56.767 --> 00:03:00.661
A safe space for Rohingya women and girls,

00:03:00.661 --> 00:03:04.890
and a Hindu-Para Center
for a small Hindu minority.

00:03:04.890 --> 00:03:07.290
The Rohingyas are mainly Muslim,

00:03:07.290 --> 00:03:08.715
but there is a tiny minority,

00:03:08.715 --> 00:03:11.712
and the Hindu-Para Center is
extremely important, because it

00:03:11.712 --> 00:03:15.270
is shared with the Bangladeshi community,

00:03:15.270 --> 00:03:17.850
so with the host community.

00:03:17.850 --> 00:03:22.110
What stands out of this
collaboration with the refugees,

00:03:22.110 --> 00:03:25.760
is a work that is utterly
sophisticated and known

00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:28.950
for the beauty, the exquisite beauty

00:03:28.950 --> 00:03:30.802
of the detailing and the sense of color

00:03:30.802 --> 00:03:34.470
and joy that emerges from these projects.

00:03:34.470 --> 00:03:37.410
But you have to see them for
yourself, and no one is more

00:03:37.410 --> 00:03:42.060
knowledgeable and more eloquent
in explicating his own work

00:03:42.060 --> 00:03:44.160
than Rizvi Hassan himself.

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:47.350
So in the name of everybody
who's attending this lecture,

00:03:47.350 --> 00:03:50.400
I wish to welcome Rizvi Hassan to Yale.

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:53.640
It is a real honor to have
you speak to us today.

00:03:53.640 --> 00:03:55.049
Thank you.

00:03:55.049 --> 00:03:56.077
- Thank you so much, Esther,

00:03:56.077 --> 00:04:00.114
for introducing me with such kind words,

00:04:00.114 --> 00:04:01.920
I'm flattered to be here.

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:06.407
And I'd like to thank the whole
platform who organized this

00:04:06.407 --> 00:04:09.795
and arranged this scope of discussion

00:04:09.795 --> 00:04:14.103
between a larger community and this one.

00:04:15.134 --> 00:04:20.134
So basically what I'm gonna try
to do here today is share my

00:04:21.684 --> 00:04:23.550
experience with the Rohingya refugees

00:04:23.550 --> 00:04:25.110
in the past five years.

00:04:25.110 --> 00:04:28.353
So what we have done,
how we get to know them,

00:04:28.353 --> 00:04:31.470
and what were our
observations and learning.

00:04:31.470 --> 00:04:34.890
I mean, working with them has been

00:04:34.890 --> 00:04:36.780
a massive change of our own

00:04:36.780 --> 00:04:39.000
philosophy, ambition of life as well.

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:42.446
And I'll just try to share this with you,

00:04:42.446 --> 00:04:44.977
and I'd very much like to welcome

00:04:44.977 --> 00:04:48.330
if anyone has any
questions or observations,

00:04:48.330 --> 00:04:50.583
any kind of understanding,

00:04:50.583 --> 00:04:54.810
please do share and we
can have a discussion.

00:04:54.810 --> 00:04:56.913
I'll just share the slides.

00:04:56.913 --> 00:05:01.913
So you know, Bangladesh
is a delta landscape,

00:05:03.630 --> 00:05:07.053
and this is one of the
largest delta in the world.

00:05:08.010 --> 00:05:13.010
And many architects here has
been working on how really you

00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:16.503
need to work on a changing landscape.

00:05:17.580 --> 00:05:22.580
Our whole country is
literally created on silt,

00:05:23.836 --> 00:05:25.950
a lot of rivers, a lot of streams,

00:05:25.950 --> 00:05:28.869
and that makes the landscape changeable.

00:05:28.869 --> 00:05:31.499
And we had the opportunity to

00:05:31.499 --> 00:05:36.499
look up on many great architects
from Bangladesh who are

00:05:36.840 --> 00:05:40.230
already practicing in the
field, and trying to figure out

00:05:40.230 --> 00:05:44.760
this new method or new trend
or new technologies that can

00:05:44.760 --> 00:05:48.366
really go with the lifestyle
of a changing landscape.

00:05:48.366 --> 00:05:50.580
And part of that, as a part of that,

00:05:50.580 --> 00:05:52.440
we have been working in
different cities like Dhaka,

00:05:52.440 --> 00:05:56.670
Jhenaidah, Cox's Bazar,
Ukhia-Teknaf, or Sylhet,

00:05:56.670 --> 00:05:58.563
and in different situations.

00:05:59.760 --> 00:06:04.760
And the part of the journey
was to really determine who we

00:06:06.537 --> 00:06:11.430
are in this landscape, and
what should be our role as

00:06:11.430 --> 00:06:15.557
architects in this country,
or in this region even.

00:06:15.557 --> 00:06:18.450
And that's how it started.

00:06:18.450 --> 00:06:23.103
And we went to the camp in 2018.

00:06:23.103 --> 00:06:27.551
I'll tell you a bit about the camp.

00:06:27.551 --> 00:06:31.620
You know, the influx happened in 2017.

00:06:31.620 --> 00:06:34.665
The big influx, around a million refugees,

00:06:34.665 --> 00:06:37.800
Rohingya refugees, that fled from Myanmar

00:06:37.800 --> 00:06:40.320
to Bangladesh overnight.

00:06:40.320 --> 00:06:45.180
And it happened before 2017 as well.

00:06:45.180 --> 00:06:47.913
But that was like in a very small scale,

00:06:48.990 --> 00:06:51.510
like 20,000 people, 30,000 people.

00:06:51.510 --> 00:06:54.573
They used to come from
1992 to this country,

00:06:54.573 --> 00:06:57.183
often in a regular basis.

00:06:58.560 --> 00:07:01.183
But this is the first time

00:07:01.183 --> 00:07:05.782
such a big influx happened, in 2017.

00:07:05.782 --> 00:07:10.410
And it was a genocide, to us,

00:07:10.410 --> 00:07:14.310
what we could see, whether
it was official or not,

00:07:14.310 --> 00:07:16.140
but it was a massive thing.

00:07:16.140 --> 00:07:21.140
And later they started
building on the Teknaf Hills.

00:07:23.520 --> 00:07:26.820
The massive camp was being
built like overnight.

00:07:26.820 --> 00:07:31.820
You see, this was actually
reserved forest of Bangladesh,

00:07:33.060 --> 00:07:34.505
what you see here.

00:07:34.505 --> 00:07:39.505
And overnight, within weeks,
two weeks or three weeks,

00:07:40.467 --> 00:07:44.400
these old trees were gone,
and they were just building

00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:48.093
shelters, toilets, to support
this massive community.

00:07:48.960 --> 00:07:51.543
And slowly it started growing like a city.

00:07:52.397 --> 00:07:57.397
So what we observed is
that from 2017, to 2018,

00:07:58.550 --> 00:08:02.880
it was kind of an emergency
phase, where everyone really

00:08:02.880 --> 00:08:04.920
rushed and give their everything

00:08:04.920 --> 00:08:07.950
to support with the basic facilities.

00:08:07.950 --> 00:08:11.160
I mean, there was no roofs,
there was no wash facilities.

00:08:11.160 --> 00:08:15.099
People couldn't understand if
they're walking on mud, or was

00:08:15.099 --> 00:08:17.670
it human waste or...

00:08:17.670 --> 00:08:20.130
it was fairly messy situation.

00:08:20.130 --> 00:08:23.940
I mean, 1 million people was a big deal.

00:08:23.940 --> 00:08:27.380
And from 2018 to 2019,

00:08:27.380 --> 00:08:32.071
it was mostly like a
post-emergency phase, where

00:08:32.071 --> 00:08:37.071
it was more about how we can
organize the whole camp now,

00:08:37.856 --> 00:08:41.563
in terms of planning,
road networks, facilities,

00:08:41.563 --> 00:08:42.690
all sort of thing.

00:08:42.690 --> 00:08:44.340
And then the development phase,

00:08:44.340 --> 00:08:46.227
I mean we observed a
lot of things going on,

00:08:46.227 --> 00:08:49.800
like a service mentioning mental health,

00:08:49.800 --> 00:08:53.063
health facility, and
your identity, culture.

00:08:53.063 --> 00:08:57.240
It was all slowly being recognized

00:08:57.240 --> 00:08:58.540
in this 2020, '21 and '22.

00:09:03.240 --> 00:09:07.612
So this is a photo from Jhenaidah.

00:09:07.612 --> 00:09:11.010
We worked before that camp here,

00:09:11.010 --> 00:09:16.010
and we observed many
people working together.

00:09:16.050 --> 00:09:19.080
Like, there is no architect in this photo,

00:09:19.080 --> 00:09:22.380
but how they really plan the city together

00:09:22.380 --> 00:09:24.810
with the help of one or two architects.

00:09:24.810 --> 00:09:26.517
It was very fascinating to us,

00:09:26.517 --> 00:09:29.610
and we learned it from
Khandaker Hassibul Kabir,

00:09:29.610 --> 00:09:33.663
another architect who is
our mentor, and he does this

00:09:33.663 --> 00:09:37.270
collaboration with general
people, or all sort of

00:09:37.270 --> 00:09:42.270
community members and try to
build the environment together.

00:09:42.960 --> 00:09:47.863
And we learned from that,
and we tried to do the same,

00:09:49.265 --> 00:09:53.040
almost do the same in
the refugee camp context,

00:09:53.040 --> 00:09:55.260
as we were very much fortunate to see

00:09:55.260 --> 00:09:57.300
that process in front of our eye.

00:09:57.300 --> 00:10:00.552
And later we were able to apply that

00:10:00.552 --> 00:10:03.450
in the camp situation as well.

00:10:03.450 --> 00:10:05.130
So the people you see here,

00:10:05.130 --> 00:10:09.570
these are all our team
members in the last few years.

00:10:09.570 --> 00:10:14.280
We found a lot of people
who were working there,

00:10:14.280 --> 00:10:18.090
rushing to support the whole situation.

00:10:18.090 --> 00:10:23.090
And they were very passionate
people who wanted to support

00:10:24.420 --> 00:10:27.630
anyhow, with whatever they have in hand.

00:10:27.630 --> 00:10:31.238
It didn't matter if you were
a doctor or an architect or

00:10:31.238 --> 00:10:34.166
whichever professionally you were,

00:10:34.166 --> 00:10:38.250
everyone was there to do
anything, just day and night.

00:10:38.250 --> 00:10:41.490
And we became friends to them.

00:10:41.490 --> 00:10:44.880
We designed a few facilities
for the situation,

00:10:44.880 --> 00:10:47.700
and many things went wrong
and many things went right.

00:10:47.700 --> 00:10:52.680
We started learning who
the refugee community is,

00:10:52.680 --> 00:10:54.080
who the Rohingya people are.

00:10:55.650 --> 00:10:59.040
It took us almost six months to

00:10:59.040 --> 00:11:02.780
really understand the people there,

00:11:02.780 --> 00:11:05.670
to really understand the
sociopolitical scenario

00:11:05.670 --> 00:11:07.140
there and what's going on,

00:11:07.140 --> 00:11:11.010
where it should go to
understand the government rules,

00:11:11.010 --> 00:11:11.970
what should be done,

00:11:11.970 --> 00:11:13.681
and what should be the ethical side

00:11:13.681 --> 00:11:15.573
of doing what we are doing.

00:11:16.410 --> 00:11:21.410
And slowly, we tried to
experiment with many projects,

00:11:22.756 --> 00:11:26.220
that we did in a very softer approach.

00:11:26.220 --> 00:11:30.030
Like we didn't want anything heavy

00:11:30.030 --> 00:11:31.442
or anything big or massive

00:11:31.442 --> 00:11:34.620
in the early phase because we could

00:11:34.620 --> 00:11:35.970
do a lot of mistakes there.

00:11:37.200 --> 00:11:42.200
So this is a project, this is
a project for Rohingya women.

00:11:42.579 --> 00:11:44.820
It's in camp 25.

00:11:44.820 --> 00:11:49.820
It's called Safe Space for
Rohingya Refugee Women and Girls.

00:11:51.630 --> 00:11:55.581
This is the first project where
we could really collaborate

00:11:55.581 --> 00:11:58.740
with the artisans, with the refugees.

00:11:58.740 --> 00:12:03.481
And it was the first practice
to build some space together.

00:12:03.481 --> 00:12:08.481
What you see here is a
mix, it's a camp, again,

00:12:09.900 --> 00:12:11.880
it's a host community as well.

00:12:11.880 --> 00:12:14.070
I mean you see a lot of shades,

00:12:14.070 --> 00:12:17.400
the blue or the white shades,

00:12:17.400 --> 00:12:20.733
these are mostly the
Bangladeshi community,

00:12:21.660 --> 00:12:23.550
the hosting nationals.

00:12:23.550 --> 00:12:28.550
And you will see a lot of
shelters within this context.

00:12:28.920 --> 00:12:32.129
Like this is a camp where
there's a mixed of host community

00:12:32.129 --> 00:12:35.190
and the refugee shelters,
like for example,

00:12:35.190 --> 00:12:38.791
on the right top side, these
are the refugee shelters,

00:12:38.791 --> 00:12:42.570
the dense ones are the refugee
shelters, that sort of thing.

00:12:42.570 --> 00:12:47.570
And what happened was we got
to know from our friends from

00:12:47.970 --> 00:12:51.084
the GBV team, Gender Based Violence who

00:12:51.084 --> 00:12:53.066
deals with gender based violence that

00:12:53.066 --> 00:12:58.066
a lot of problems are rising,
because of this mix of

00:12:59.073 --> 00:13:00.956
these two communities.

00:13:00.956 --> 00:13:05.280
A lot of violence, a lot of
threats, a lot of conflicts,

00:13:05.280 --> 00:13:08.550
especially on women and girls,

00:13:08.550 --> 00:13:13.380
that was coming to the scenario.

00:13:13.380 --> 00:13:16.440
And we were working
with BRAC at that time,

00:13:16.440 --> 00:13:18.617
and BRAC decided to deal

00:13:18.617 --> 00:13:21.240
with the situation and they said, "Okay,

00:13:21.240 --> 00:13:25.121
maybe we can sit with them and
try to consult what we can do

00:13:25.121 --> 00:13:29.610
and maybe create a platform
where we can actually share

00:13:29.610 --> 00:13:31.410
these thoughts and share this conflicts

00:13:31.410 --> 00:13:36.150
and try to deal with in a
legal way or professional way.

00:13:36.150 --> 00:13:41.150
And this was the space or structure

00:13:44.051 --> 00:13:45.989
that we designed with them.

00:13:45.989 --> 00:13:50.989
It was suggested, or the
communication was made with the

00:13:51.030 --> 00:13:55.320
women that what they want to feel safe.

00:13:55.320 --> 00:13:59.180
So they said, okay, the
camp is very brutal and

00:13:59.180 --> 00:14:04.180
they don't feel safe or
relaxed in any room, any space.

00:14:06.447 --> 00:14:08.670
You see all the boxy shapes there.

00:14:08.670 --> 00:14:11.180
They don't feel safe there,
because people peek through,

00:14:11.180 --> 00:14:14.190
and you can see everything,
what's going on,

00:14:14.190 --> 00:14:15.393
they can hear you.

00:14:16.350 --> 00:14:17.940
So what we suggested, okay,

00:14:17.940 --> 00:14:22.940
maybe we can create an interior
court, open up to the inside

00:14:23.171 --> 00:14:27.255
and which is very colorful and
vibrant, and there are a lot

00:14:27.255 --> 00:14:30.060
of layers surrounding to it.

00:14:30.060 --> 00:14:34.413
And that probably will offer
you a better sense of privacy.

00:14:35.400 --> 00:14:37.290
And this was the space.

00:14:37.290 --> 00:14:40.380
You cannot really see what's
going on inside from outside.

00:14:40.380 --> 00:14:43.346
It's a very ragged thing,
but the inside is very,

00:14:43.346 --> 00:14:45.300
you have the colors.

00:14:45.300 --> 00:14:50.300
I mean, you feel open up when
you were in the courtyard.

00:14:50.580 --> 00:14:52.800
And it was a success for us

00:14:52.800 --> 00:14:55.260
because a lot of users were coming.

00:14:55.260 --> 00:15:00.260
Even, I want to share one story
that I liked most about this

00:15:02.621 --> 00:15:06.631
structure, is that we were
observing many these sort of

00:15:06.631 --> 00:15:09.553
facilities in other camps.

00:15:09.553 --> 00:15:13.450
And what we observed mostly is that

00:15:14.473 --> 00:15:17.580
the male members of the community,

00:15:17.580 --> 00:15:21.780
they don't feel very secure
to let their female members go

00:15:21.780 --> 00:15:23.100
to such a facility.

00:15:23.100 --> 00:15:27.990
They think that it's a cultural threat,

00:15:27.990 --> 00:15:31.080
it's a religious threat, and many issues.

00:15:31.080 --> 00:15:34.704
So they always used to say
no to the female members,

00:15:34.704 --> 00:15:39.390
"Don't go to a facility,
that is run by an NGO

00:15:39.390 --> 00:15:41.790
without our permission,"
and that sort of thing.

00:15:43.077 --> 00:15:46.170
But the fun thing is when
we started building this,

00:15:46.170 --> 00:15:48.255
everyone started calling this a stadium.

00:15:48.255 --> 00:15:50.264
The the male members especially,

00:15:50.264 --> 00:15:52.567
they were crafting the
space and they said,

00:15:52.567 --> 00:15:56.730
these were the craftsmen.

00:15:56.730 --> 00:15:57.637
And they said,

00:15:57.637 --> 00:15:59.127
"Please hire us in this facility."

00:15:59.127 --> 00:15:59.960
And we said,

00:15:59.960 --> 00:16:01.410
"This is a woman facility,

00:16:01.410 --> 00:16:04.136
why do you want to be hired here?"

00:16:04.136 --> 00:16:05.943
They thought for a while
and they said, "Okay,

00:16:05.943 --> 00:16:09.594
then I'll send my mother
or my wife or my daughter

00:16:09.594 --> 00:16:13.140
to attend in this facility regularly."

00:16:13.140 --> 00:16:14.815
So this was the first
time we thought, "okay,

00:16:14.815 --> 00:16:18.973
maybe the space is actually
making some change."

00:16:18.973 --> 00:16:21.330
I mean, we didn't heard this from

00:16:21.330 --> 00:16:23.190
the male members for a long time,

00:16:23.190 --> 00:16:25.803
that they were trying
to send female members

00:16:25.803 --> 00:16:27.270
in a positive manner.

00:16:27.270 --> 00:16:30.813
But this story was a success
for me in this project.

00:16:32.970 --> 00:16:37.970
And now the host community come
here, the refugee community

00:16:39.460 --> 00:16:43.230
comes here, and they often
discuss about their problems.

00:16:43.230 --> 00:16:44.079
I mean, it's a problem.

00:16:44.079 --> 00:16:46.823
It's a lot of problem
in their regular life,

00:16:46.823 --> 00:16:51.364
but they get to have a
sense of joy in a space,

00:16:51.364 --> 00:16:54.485
in a very brutal context.

00:16:54.485 --> 00:16:58.737
Sometimes this space
turns to a green space,

00:16:58.737 --> 00:17:00.630
and they let it grow.

00:17:00.630 --> 00:17:03.213
And it's fun to watch that way.

00:17:05.880 --> 00:17:09.395
And later, this is another
project after that one,

00:17:09.395 --> 00:17:14.133
this was another project in the
Hindu-Para Community Center.

00:17:15.924 --> 00:17:18.093
So the Hindu-Para,

00:17:19.920 --> 00:17:24.060
I'll talk a little bit about
the Hindu-Para refugee camp.

00:17:24.060 --> 00:17:26.970
You see in the drawing on the right side,

00:17:26.970 --> 00:17:29.310
it's a very dense settlement.

00:17:29.310 --> 00:17:31.950
That is the small camp for the Hindu,

00:17:31.950 --> 00:17:36.003
community people who
are Hindu by religion.

00:17:36.904 --> 00:17:39.930
And in the left it's the host community,

00:17:39.930 --> 00:17:41.585
that is Muslim.

00:17:41.585 --> 00:17:45.302
So, you know, Rohingya
refugees are mostly Muslim,

00:17:45.302 --> 00:17:48.633
90%, more than 90%, they're Muslim people.

00:17:50.045 --> 00:17:54.339
But this small Hindu community happened to

00:17:54.339 --> 00:17:58.080
fled with them during the influx,

00:17:58.080 --> 00:18:01.050
and they were isolated from the main camp.

00:18:01.050 --> 00:18:06.050
So there was a conflict
regarding religion.

00:18:07.820 --> 00:18:12.820
So what we found out is
that they were more insecure

00:18:12.884 --> 00:18:16.780
in terms of like being
friends with each other and

00:18:16.780 --> 00:18:18.660
communication and everything.

00:18:18.660 --> 00:18:19.687
When we went there,

00:18:19.687 --> 00:18:22.680
they were very insecure,

00:18:22.680 --> 00:18:25.230
that they thought we're gonna again,

00:18:25.230 --> 00:18:27.608
do some harm to them,
or break their temple

00:18:27.608 --> 00:18:29.820
or that sort of thing.

00:18:29.820 --> 00:18:32.733
So we decided not to work in the camp,

00:18:32.733 --> 00:18:35.838
but just add this into
the camp, so that it is a

00:18:35.838 --> 00:18:40.838
softer approach, that we do not
directly go inside the camp,

00:18:41.430 --> 00:18:44.790
but we build something
nice and invite them,

00:18:44.790 --> 00:18:47.970
and try to build the trust
slowly in the process.

00:18:47.970 --> 00:18:49.860
And then we thought,
okay, maybe this facility

00:18:49.860 --> 00:18:52.740
can also be used by the host community

00:18:52.740 --> 00:18:54.720
when the refugees are gone.

00:18:54.720 --> 00:18:57.990
So this was a structure where
we did also a lot of technical

00:18:57.990 --> 00:19:01.320
experiments, like all
the panels are modular.

00:19:01.320 --> 00:19:06.320
You can really, dismantle
this one and shift it

00:19:06.780 --> 00:19:11.580
to some other place, or it's
less wastage technology,

00:19:11.580 --> 00:19:13.290
that very modular design.

00:19:13.290 --> 00:19:18.290
Everything is cut within the
dimensions and easy to install.

00:19:20.268 --> 00:19:23.250
So this was the before scenario.

00:19:23.250 --> 00:19:27.690
We found a abandoned shed,

00:19:27.690 --> 00:19:28.920
that was a chicken, poultry shed,

00:19:28.920 --> 00:19:33.302
but it was abandoned, and we
rented it and we converted the

00:19:33.302 --> 00:19:36.690
same exact idea to a new space.

00:19:36.690 --> 00:19:41.412
I mean, we didn't even try to
plan it or extend the rooms,

00:19:41.412 --> 00:19:44.910
whatever we had, whatever the room was,

00:19:44.910 --> 00:19:47.343
we just converted it to
a new community center.

00:19:52.227 --> 00:19:54.180
And you see the walls,

00:19:54.180 --> 00:19:56.580
these are all modular
panels that are attached,

00:19:56.580 --> 00:19:58.770
and just made, they're steel-framed.

00:19:58.770 --> 00:20:00.588
These all can be
dismantled, because the camp

00:20:00.588 --> 00:20:04.489
is a very unpredictable situation.

00:20:04.489 --> 00:20:09.217
We saw a lot of structures
being demolished within a night.

00:20:09.217 --> 00:20:11.700
The camp in charge, government official,

00:20:11.700 --> 00:20:13.710
they would come and they would say, "Okay,

00:20:13.710 --> 00:20:16.091
we have to move this from this side, and

00:20:16.091 --> 00:20:20.833
we have to make space from something else

00:20:20.833 --> 00:20:22.110
for something else."

00:20:22.110 --> 00:20:23.940
So this happened a lot.

00:20:23.940 --> 00:20:25.709
And we thought, why not experiment

00:20:25.709 --> 00:20:28.764
with some technology or some technique

00:20:28.764 --> 00:20:33.764
where we really can do
this modular solution.

00:20:34.129 --> 00:20:39.129
And this was acceptable, and
everyone loved that solution,

00:20:39.720 --> 00:20:42.685
even the donor, because the donor,

00:20:42.685 --> 00:20:44.730
they spent a lot of budget,

00:20:44.730 --> 00:20:47.250
a lot of money on these structures.

00:20:47.250 --> 00:20:49.140
But within one year or two years,

00:20:49.140 --> 00:20:50.730
these structures are like gone,

00:20:50.730 --> 00:20:52.350
and you cannot salvage anything.

00:20:52.350 --> 00:20:53.460
But in this solution,

00:20:53.460 --> 00:20:56.340
you can almost salvage everything from it.

00:20:56.340 --> 00:20:59.253
You can just use this any time you want.

00:21:02.425 --> 00:21:07.140
And you see that color, the essence,

00:21:07.140 --> 00:21:09.542
the texture was very important
for us, because we tried to

00:21:09.542 --> 00:21:14.542
connect with who there, I mean
with with their skin color,

00:21:15.417 --> 00:21:19.483
with their clothes,
what they can relate to.

00:21:19.483 --> 00:21:21.447
And it was not very difficult because

00:21:21.447 --> 00:21:26.447
we looked around for clues,
whatever was in the surrounding,

00:21:26.520 --> 00:21:28.674
and we tried to bring the solutions,

00:21:28.674 --> 00:21:31.323
and compose the solutions.

00:21:33.870 --> 00:21:38.340
And later they had fun.

00:21:38.340 --> 00:21:41.699
When it was inaugurating,
they were painting the space,

00:21:41.699 --> 00:21:45.670
they were enjoying the
colors and everything.

00:21:45.670 --> 00:21:48.810
And in a similar technique,

00:21:48.810 --> 00:21:52.177
we built a lot of structures
for the hosting villages as

00:21:52.177 --> 00:21:55.683
well, for the Bangladeshi
community, not just the refugees.

00:21:56.612 --> 00:21:59.671
Because it was also about balancing,

00:21:59.671 --> 00:22:04.671
balancing the conflict or peace

00:22:05.460 --> 00:22:07.050
between these two communities.

00:22:07.050 --> 00:22:09.630
Because the host community,

00:22:09.630 --> 00:22:11.847
they were addressing
these issues that, okay,

00:22:11.847 --> 00:22:13.606
the refugees are getting
a lot of facilities

00:22:13.606 --> 00:22:16.230
because of their influx,

00:22:16.230 --> 00:22:18.390
where the host communities are still poor

00:22:18.390 --> 00:22:20.010
and they don't get anything.

00:22:20.010 --> 00:22:22.140
And the government had
this rule that okay,

00:22:22.140 --> 00:22:25.788
maybe 25% to 30% fund will be spent

00:22:25.788 --> 00:22:27.812
for the hosting nationals.

00:22:27.812 --> 00:22:32.812
And this was one project that
we did for them among several.

00:22:39.000 --> 00:22:40.230
And in the meantime,

00:22:40.230 --> 00:22:43.450
we also supported the
government with our solutions.

00:22:43.450 --> 00:22:46.890
This was a government
building on the left that was

00:22:46.890 --> 00:22:48.870
converted in a very short time.

00:22:48.870 --> 00:22:52.173
Probably took three months
to design and construct.

00:22:53.310 --> 00:22:55.173
Now all the meetings,

00:22:55.173 --> 00:23:00.173
all the ideation or decisions
are made in this building.

00:23:01.796 --> 00:23:04.853
This project helped us build
trust with the government.

00:23:04.853 --> 00:23:07.380
I mean they started understanding, okay,

00:23:07.380 --> 00:23:08.855
an architect can offer something in

00:23:08.855 --> 00:23:13.230
a very short time period.

00:23:13.230 --> 00:23:15.380
And they don't take
like two years to design

00:23:15.380 --> 00:23:18.827
and construct for three years now.

00:23:18.827 --> 00:23:23.223
But this is not very perfect,
but this helped us a lot.

00:23:25.380 --> 00:23:27.359
And these are few projects we did

00:23:27.359 --> 00:23:31.344
later in the same technique.

00:23:31.344 --> 00:23:35.673
We also did some experiments
where we used to live,

00:23:35.673 --> 00:23:38.233
this is the left home, you see,

00:23:38.233 --> 00:23:41.976
we converted it to the
right one, the left home,

00:23:41.976 --> 00:23:44.794
the owner, he drives the car.

00:23:44.794 --> 00:23:47.850
He's from the community where we

00:23:47.850 --> 00:23:49.560
used to live and we thought, okay,

00:23:49.560 --> 00:23:51.253
maybe we do a little bit experiment

00:23:51.253 --> 00:23:53.393
here in the host community.

00:23:54.365 --> 00:23:58.107
And we did the space, and turned
it into a studio like this.

00:23:58.107 --> 00:23:59.760
And he was very happy.

00:23:59.760 --> 00:24:02.013
And he's a friend of ours now.

00:24:07.170 --> 00:24:12.170
this is one project that we did almost

00:24:16.170 --> 00:24:20.133
in the last phase of the camp development.

00:24:22.350 --> 00:24:25.200
I mean, after working
in many other centers

00:24:25.200 --> 00:24:30.200
or many other camps, UN IOM, UN migration,

00:24:30.750 --> 00:24:35.750
they decided that they wanted
to deal with the identity and

00:24:37.020 --> 00:24:40.890
cultural issue of the Rohingya community.

00:24:40.890 --> 00:24:44.850
And this was a project
under the mental health and

00:24:44.850 --> 00:24:47.883
psychosocial support unit of IOM.

00:24:49.067 --> 00:24:54.067
It started, with few cultural activities

00:24:55.530 --> 00:24:57.183
or creative activities,

00:24:58.200 --> 00:25:01.110
and they tried to figure out who

00:25:01.110 --> 00:25:04.020
the Rohingya communities
are with their products,

00:25:04.020 --> 00:25:06.840
with their techniques,
bamboo weaving, basketry,

00:25:06.840 --> 00:25:08.103
which are stories, oral stories,

00:25:08.103 --> 00:25:12.517
and stories from children, the elderly.

00:25:12.517 --> 00:25:17.517
And they discovered that
this is a huge thing.

00:25:18.150 --> 00:25:21.060
I mean their stories, their culture,

00:25:21.060 --> 00:25:21.893
their identity is something

00:25:21.893 --> 00:25:24.720
that we are completely overlooking.

00:25:24.720 --> 00:25:25.920
We are just building shelter, shelter,

00:25:25.920 --> 00:25:27.273
shelter, shelter for them.

00:25:29.130 --> 00:25:32.680
But we are actually not trying
to understand that they are

00:25:32.680 --> 00:25:37.680
actually a community that
has their own unique identity

00:25:38.310 --> 00:25:39.300
and a lot of stories,

00:25:39.300 --> 00:25:43.170
lot of skills, that they
can share with the work.

00:25:43.170 --> 00:25:45.810
And at that time, Manuel Marques Pereira,

00:25:45.810 --> 00:25:48.600
who was the Chief of Mission from IOM,

00:25:48.600 --> 00:25:53.031
he was a very efficient
person, and he and the team,

00:25:53.031 --> 00:25:56.918
they formulated this project, said, "Okay,

00:25:56.918 --> 00:26:01.084
maybe we do a project that
really changed the idea of

00:26:01.084 --> 00:26:04.113
Rohingya community
completely to the world,

00:26:05.760 --> 00:26:07.650
how we can do that?"

00:26:07.650 --> 00:26:10.110
So we were involved at that time,

00:26:10.110 --> 00:26:13.860
and we were called and we

00:26:13.860 --> 00:26:18.515
started the ideation, okay,

00:26:18.515 --> 00:26:22.230
how we really can involve
and really can understand.

00:26:22.230 --> 00:26:27.230
And so what we did was we
did a lot of workshops and

00:26:27.240 --> 00:26:30.030
one-to-one interactions and
person-to-person interactions

00:26:30.030 --> 00:26:33.450
and with elderly, with the children,

00:26:33.450 --> 00:26:36.900
with the female group, community
leaders, and really try to

00:26:36.900 --> 00:26:39.960
understand their stories,
as simple as that.

00:26:39.960 --> 00:26:43.350
And we entered some design workshops.

00:26:43.350 --> 00:26:45.338
It's in the camp, you see the back

00:26:45.338 --> 00:26:50.338
wall is almost like turning down.

00:26:51.510 --> 00:26:53.880
That is almost breaking apart.

00:26:53.880 --> 00:26:55.054
But this was our shape.

00:26:55.054 --> 00:26:57.270
This was our office space in the camp.

00:26:57.270 --> 00:27:00.309
And these were all the
refugee volunteers there.

00:27:00.309 --> 00:27:02.784
And many were carpenter,

00:27:02.784 --> 00:27:04.798
many were school teachers

00:27:04.798 --> 00:27:07.830
and university teacher from the Myanmar.

00:27:07.830 --> 00:27:12.244
And they started gathering through

00:27:12.244 --> 00:27:16.082
this team, and they shared
all sort of idea, history,

00:27:16.082 --> 00:27:18.135
all sort of information.

00:27:18.135 --> 00:27:22.260
And you see the boat
there, they made it here.

00:27:22.260 --> 00:27:25.140
And this was made by (indistinct)

00:27:25.140 --> 00:27:26.580
you see the old guy here, (chuckles)

00:27:26.580 --> 00:27:29.674
he's a very tough type of person,

00:27:29.674 --> 00:27:31.047
a very hotheaded person,

00:27:31.047 --> 00:27:33.570
and he makes the most beautiful boats

00:27:33.570 --> 00:27:35.823
you can imagine in the community.

00:27:37.580 --> 00:27:42.240
So we started having fun with these guys,

00:27:42.240 --> 00:27:43.680
having design session with these guys.

00:27:43.680 --> 00:27:45.900
I always remember that how we had

00:27:45.900 --> 00:27:47.630
classes in the universities.

00:27:47.630 --> 00:27:52.230
Like I'm from the University
of Engineering and Technology.

00:27:52.230 --> 00:27:57.230
I mean, I always tried to
replicate that in the camp.

00:27:57.900 --> 00:28:00.120
I mean they don't have to
be the university students,

00:28:00.120 --> 00:28:03.336
but we can have the same sort
of knowledge, sharing things.

00:28:03.336 --> 00:28:07.620
We can have classes, we
can have studio, tools,

00:28:07.620 --> 00:28:09.366
we can do things together.

00:28:09.366 --> 00:28:11.527
And it was not very expensive because

00:28:11.527 --> 00:28:12.360
they are carpenters,

00:28:12.360 --> 00:28:15.540
they have the tools and
they have wastage materials,

00:28:15.540 --> 00:28:19.070
and they used to make this out of quite...

00:28:20.340 --> 00:28:23.900
it didn't need much budget for that.

00:28:23.900 --> 00:28:28.900
And we found out a lot what they can make

00:28:30.510 --> 00:28:32.490
and what they can draw.

00:28:32.490 --> 00:28:35.670
What were their imagination like.

00:28:35.670 --> 00:28:40.670
In one studio day we asked
them to design a panel,

00:28:40.710 --> 00:28:43.800
a wall that no one has ever seen.

00:28:43.800 --> 00:28:46.140
I mean we didn't say anything but just,

00:28:46.140 --> 00:28:48.960
we said "Design a weird wall."

00:28:48.960 --> 00:28:53.078
And this is a drawing
by Zaber (indistinct)

00:28:53.078 --> 00:28:54.395
He's an artisan.

00:28:54.395 --> 00:28:58.170
So he made his panels from the,

00:28:58.170 --> 00:28:59.520
that is a window panel, but

00:28:59.520 --> 00:29:03.236
that can be converted to a
table and a chair, you know?

00:29:03.236 --> 00:29:07.654
So the wall is not just a
wall, it's also functional.

00:29:07.654 --> 00:29:11.760
And we didn't ask for this model from him,

00:29:11.760 --> 00:29:13.110
but he made it either way.

00:29:13.110 --> 00:29:14.280
I don't know why.

00:29:14.280 --> 00:29:17.361
But that excited
everyone, that said, okay,

00:29:17.361 --> 00:29:20.884
we are dealing with something
very exclusive here,

00:29:20.884 --> 00:29:23.463
and passionate people here.

00:29:25.944 --> 00:29:27.540
This was his portfolio.

00:29:27.540 --> 00:29:30.753
You can see his name is
written in the journal.

00:29:33.660 --> 00:29:37.500
So we try to find out stories,

00:29:37.500 --> 00:29:42.500
things, even within the
group of IOM in our office,

00:29:45.570 --> 00:29:49.950
we try to gather as much
information as we could,

00:29:49.950 --> 00:29:54.450
because when we are dealing
with the community and

00:29:54.450 --> 00:29:58.560
we're saying, okay, it's a
Rohingya cultural memory center,

00:29:58.560 --> 00:30:02.493
I'm not the person to
decide who is Rohingya.

00:30:04.341 --> 00:30:05.970
The information that I'll get,

00:30:05.970 --> 00:30:09.030
if I can compose that or assemble that,

00:30:09.030 --> 00:30:11.610
or we can make use of that for betterment,

00:30:11.610 --> 00:30:14.970
probably that will give some
sense of their identity.

00:30:14.970 --> 00:30:16.501
Not all, but maybe some sense.

00:30:16.501 --> 00:30:19.974
So we try to gather a lot of information,

00:30:19.974 --> 00:30:24.090
and anyone's idea, anyone's thought,

00:30:24.090 --> 00:30:27.949
anyone's comment, and we found out

00:30:27.949 --> 00:30:29.869
many techniques that they use.

00:30:29.869 --> 00:30:33.990
Again, workshops, you
see that on the right,

00:30:33.990 --> 00:30:35.818
these are our master artisans teaching

00:30:35.818 --> 00:30:40.470
to their students the nipa
palm technique for the roofing.

00:30:40.470 --> 00:30:42.900
This technique is almost lost.

00:30:42.900 --> 00:30:47.700
I mean, in Bangladesh you
cannot find it anymore,

00:30:47.700 --> 00:30:51.213
but we try to revive this through them.

00:30:52.440 --> 00:30:53.730
And this is very effective.

00:30:53.730 --> 00:30:55.590
This technique is actually very effective.

00:30:55.590 --> 00:31:00.590
I mean, we use thatch in
many roofs, but the thatch,

00:31:03.420 --> 00:31:07.770
the problem is that has
to be replaced every year.

00:31:07.770 --> 00:31:11.280
I mean every year you have to
do this investment to replace

00:31:11.280 --> 00:31:12.790
the whole roofing system.

00:31:12.790 --> 00:31:17.790
But this technique, the nipa, it lasts.

00:31:18.180 --> 00:31:20.460
It can last for 10 years.

00:31:20.460 --> 00:31:22.940
I mean if you can tie it up properly,

00:31:22.940 --> 00:31:24.223
it can last for 10 years.

00:31:24.223 --> 00:31:26.850
So we said okay, it's a big facility.

00:31:26.850 --> 00:31:29.670
We cannot just change
the material every year.

00:31:29.670 --> 00:31:32.877
We have to use this.

00:31:32.877 --> 00:31:34.623
And it also goes in that culture.

00:31:38.653 --> 00:31:39.657
And lot of simulations happen.

00:31:39.657 --> 00:31:41.732
You see on the left side,

00:31:41.732 --> 00:31:46.732
this is a drawing by an
artisan, we asked him to

00:31:48.920 --> 00:31:53.280
think of a meditative
space or a peaceful space.

00:31:53.280 --> 00:31:55.814
While he will find peace in the camp,

00:31:55.814 --> 00:31:58.560
he has drawn this plan, you know,

00:31:58.560 --> 00:32:00.630
it has a lot of layer like the veranda,

00:32:00.630 --> 00:32:03.056
the semi-outer space.

00:32:03.056 --> 00:32:06.240
And there are rooms,
there is another space.

00:32:06.240 --> 00:32:09.330
And in the middle there is a courtyard

00:32:09.330 --> 00:32:10.586
where you can see the light.

00:32:10.586 --> 00:32:14.513
So subconsciously in our original design,

00:32:14.513 --> 00:32:17.700
it translated somehow.

00:32:17.700 --> 00:32:21.933
And you see the space now like this.

00:32:22.876 --> 00:32:27.876
So the idea of this space is
it's kind of a living museum.

00:32:28.290 --> 00:32:31.020
People come here, they have trainings,

00:32:31.020 --> 00:32:35.400
they talk to each other, they
see the cultural objects.

00:32:35.400 --> 00:32:39.300
There is a display, changeable
display all the time.

00:32:39.300 --> 00:32:42.098
And it changes time to time
and there are some permanent

00:32:42.098 --> 00:32:44.802
exhibit, you know, so it's sort of

00:32:44.802 --> 00:32:48.510
a breathable space for us within the camp.

00:32:48.510 --> 00:32:53.510
I don't know if it really
fits the definition of museum,

00:32:53.896 --> 00:32:58.505
but it's a breathable space for us.

00:32:58.505 --> 00:33:01.800
And the process, it was class every day,

00:33:01.800 --> 00:33:04.427
every day it was a fun class to attend.

00:33:06.281 --> 00:33:11.281
And we also had concerts. (laughs)

00:33:12.868 --> 00:33:16.350
So some Rohingya singers,

00:33:16.350 --> 00:33:20.430
they're singing to
entertain the craftsmen,

00:33:20.430 --> 00:33:22.178
the construction workers.

00:33:22.178 --> 00:33:26.806
And that lifted everyone's
spirit when we said, "Look,

00:33:26.806 --> 00:33:29.310
this is a Rohingya cultural memory center,

00:33:29.310 --> 00:33:34.310
and you need to come
up with all the ideas.

00:33:34.890 --> 00:33:37.689
I am not the one who is
gonna tell you what to do.

00:33:37.689 --> 00:33:40.363
I can compose with you, I can help,

00:33:40.363 --> 00:33:43.110
but you need to come up with ideas."

00:33:43.110 --> 00:33:48.110
And they came up with the
most absurd ideas you can see,

00:33:48.120 --> 00:33:50.850
and most beautiful ideas,
most with their technique,

00:33:50.850 --> 00:33:54.210
what they can do
precisely with the bamboo,

00:33:54.210 --> 00:33:58.050
with the cane, with the
wood, with the mud, with art.

00:33:58.050 --> 00:34:01.533
And they just drafted the whole thing.

00:34:11.760 --> 00:34:16.760
This is the small wall of
memories, or wall of windows.

00:34:17.540 --> 00:34:22.540
Every window is made by
an individual artisan.

00:34:23.640 --> 00:34:27.690
And that window says about
their story or memory.

00:34:27.690 --> 00:34:30.150
You can open a lot of
panel and you will see

00:34:30.150 --> 00:34:34.115
a small flower, cow, the rice field.

00:34:34.115 --> 00:34:37.980
And that says a lot of
stories in one world,

00:34:37.980 --> 00:34:40.802
to be shared with a young generation

00:34:40.802 --> 00:34:44.160
who is being born in the camp,

00:34:44.160 --> 00:34:48.477
never seen their past life in Myanmar.

00:34:48.477 --> 00:34:51.210
And they can just somehow relate to

00:34:51.210 --> 00:34:54.933
the oral stories that the elderly shares.

00:35:00.270 --> 00:35:01.170
This is Idris Bai.

00:35:06.203 --> 00:35:07.786
That is Hassan Bai.

00:35:08.803 --> 00:35:11.529
Hassan Bai made me a table.

00:35:11.529 --> 00:35:15.360
This is a very portable table.

00:35:15.360 --> 00:35:16.639
You can fold this one,

00:35:16.639 --> 00:35:19.470
you can carry this on your shoulder,

00:35:19.470 --> 00:35:22.313
take to construction sites,
put your laptop and work there.

00:35:22.313 --> 00:35:25.350
Like he was observing
that I work on floors

00:35:25.350 --> 00:35:27.930
or on on a wooden panel, whatever I get.

00:35:27.930 --> 00:35:31.169
And come bring my laptop there.

00:35:31.169 --> 00:35:34.047
And he said, "okay,
I'll make you a table."

00:35:34.047 --> 00:35:37.020
And this was such a beautiful table.

00:35:37.020 --> 00:35:38.730
I have never seen something like this.

00:35:38.730 --> 00:35:41.733
And I now carry it everywhere, almost.

00:35:49.680 --> 00:35:52.203
Is at the final drafted spaces.

00:35:59.010 --> 00:36:01.867
That is Imam (indistinct)

00:36:03.060 --> 00:36:05.430
And the beautiful thing is whenever

00:36:05.430 --> 00:36:08.010
we go to these spaces now,

00:36:08.010 --> 00:36:11.040
I mean, they sometimes say they just

00:36:11.040 --> 00:36:12.728
come to this space for no reason.

00:36:12.728 --> 00:36:14.767
And they come here, they sit,

00:36:14.767 --> 00:36:19.767
they talk to each other
because one shelter is very...

00:36:20.967 --> 00:36:25.286
it's so competitive for
like 20 family members

00:36:25.286 --> 00:36:27.200
or 15 family members.

00:36:27.200 --> 00:36:31.350
It's not very, I mean,

00:36:31.350 --> 00:36:34.057
it doesn't bring you much joy in a complex

00:36:34.057 --> 00:36:39.057
space often, or sometimes
it may stress you out.

00:36:39.420 --> 00:36:42.870
So they come with their family
members or alone to the space

00:36:42.870 --> 00:36:46.443
to have a sense of clean
and sense of light.

00:36:53.711 --> 00:36:55.980
And in the middle that was Dimitrov,

00:36:55.980 --> 00:37:00.153
He is a psychologist, he's an Ukrainian.

00:37:01.860 --> 00:37:04.317
And we had a lot of conversations as well,

00:37:04.317 --> 00:37:08.910
how we can really connect
with mental health,

00:37:08.910 --> 00:37:10.590
this creative process.

00:37:10.590 --> 00:37:13.590
And now he's in Ukraine.

00:37:13.590 --> 00:37:17.066
He had to leave the scenario
before the construction was

00:37:17.066 --> 00:37:20.880
over, but he was observing
the whole process.

00:37:20.880 --> 00:37:25.880
And he said that this is the
first time he saw a creative

00:37:25.950 --> 00:37:27.660
process or architecture really

00:37:27.660 --> 00:37:29.940
has to do something with mental health,

00:37:29.940 --> 00:37:31.140
and it really connects.

00:37:31.140 --> 00:37:34.080
I mean, when everyone was
enjoying singing, he said, "Okay,

00:37:34.080 --> 00:37:35.533
this is something you can really measure,

00:37:35.533 --> 00:37:37.560
there is no doubt."

00:37:37.560 --> 00:37:42.420
And we send him photos
(chuckles) in WhatsApp.

00:37:42.420 --> 00:37:45.818
I hope he's well, his family as well.

00:37:45.818 --> 00:37:49.953
But remotely, the photos make him smile.

00:37:54.090 --> 00:37:58.273
And yeah, this space now is actually

00:38:01.830 --> 00:38:02.897
a very important one because

00:38:02.897 --> 00:38:07.110
almost all the officials, all
the most important officials,

00:38:07.110 --> 00:38:07.950
the government officials,

00:38:07.950 --> 00:38:10.223
they come here whenever to visit the camp.

00:38:10.223 --> 00:38:13.830
They definitely come to the
Rohingya culture memory center,

00:38:13.830 --> 00:38:16.020
to daily understand who are Rohingya.

00:38:16.020 --> 00:38:19.454
And now they have a complete
different sense about this

00:38:19.454 --> 00:38:21.788
whole community that okay,

00:38:21.788 --> 00:38:25.300
this whole community is not
just a wild community or

00:38:25.300 --> 00:38:27.840
someone who cannot do anything.

00:38:27.840 --> 00:38:31.860
No, they can do a lot of thing
and they cannot be neglected.

00:38:31.860 --> 00:38:35.250
The world has to take this community

00:38:35.250 --> 00:38:36.603
seriously, no matter what.

00:38:39.531 --> 00:38:42.930
And later we managed,

00:38:42.930 --> 00:38:46.410
we worked with many other organizations

00:38:46.410 --> 00:38:48.810
like some NG forum here.

00:38:48.810 --> 00:38:53.070
And this was in our villages,
in Bangladeshi communities.

00:38:53.070 --> 00:38:55.800
And we really, what we
really observed or understood

00:38:55.800 --> 00:38:57.770
is that migration,

00:38:57.770 --> 00:39:00.716
maybe that was an international migration,

00:39:00.716 --> 00:39:05.640
or what we were dealing
about what the refugees.

00:39:05.640 --> 00:39:09.795
But internal migration is
a big issue in Bangladesh.

00:39:09.795 --> 00:39:14.580
People are leaving their
homes and villages.

00:39:14.580 --> 00:39:17.760
They don't have much clue what
to do in villages anymore.

00:39:17.760 --> 00:39:19.650
And they try to get to the city.

00:39:19.650 --> 00:39:22.590
The city is a mess, messy situation.

00:39:22.590 --> 00:39:24.123
There are slums.

00:39:25.770 --> 00:39:30.770
I mean in a lot of case this
is worse than refugee context.

00:39:31.054 --> 00:39:33.060
There's no competition, definitely.

00:39:33.060 --> 00:39:36.840
But in a lot of case, people don't care

00:39:36.840 --> 00:39:39.008
about the villagers who go to the slums,

00:39:39.008 --> 00:39:42.960
stay unhealthy, no life.

00:39:42.960 --> 00:39:47.156
I mean, so now what we are trying to do

00:39:47.156 --> 00:39:49.744
is we are trying to
enter into this villages,

00:39:49.744 --> 00:39:54.744
and trying to create
some sort of example that

00:39:54.933 --> 00:39:59.296
will tie the people with where they are.

00:39:59.296 --> 00:40:01.140
That will create good examples

00:40:01.140 --> 00:40:04.932
in the community and build on the place.

00:40:04.932 --> 00:40:09.540
You don't need to really
shift for life to the city.

00:40:09.540 --> 00:40:12.420
Yes, you can go to the
city, you can go visit,

00:40:12.420 --> 00:40:14.790
you can go for different facilities,

00:40:14.790 --> 00:40:17.139
but you don't need to leave
what you have for such a

00:40:17.139 --> 00:40:18.862
beautiful landscape you have.

00:40:18.862 --> 00:40:23.862
And how we can really create
good examples in the villages.

00:40:24.840 --> 00:40:27.180
That is our prime goal now.

00:40:27.180 --> 00:40:28.180
And we are working on that.

00:40:28.180 --> 00:40:33.180
We're trying to plan with
them, share with people.

00:40:33.397 --> 00:40:38.040
This is a corner from one same workshop,

00:40:38.040 --> 00:40:42.967
like in the morning the kid
was doing this, and in the hall

00:40:42.967 --> 00:40:46.020
this guy was like, they
were doing the same thing.

00:40:46.020 --> 00:40:49.950
And we were surprised
to see the similarities

00:40:49.950 --> 00:40:52.560
of these people, the kids and the people.

00:40:52.560 --> 00:40:54.658
So I thought, yes, everyone enjoys this,

00:40:54.658 --> 00:40:57.390
the drawing, the building together.

00:40:57.390 --> 00:41:02.390
And, in last year there
was a flood in Sylhet area.

00:41:05.899 --> 00:41:09.070
We went there for a very short time,

00:41:09.070 --> 00:41:13.638
tried to understand what is
happening, what can be done,

00:41:13.638 --> 00:41:17.840
and tried to learn a
lot from them as well.

00:41:17.840 --> 00:41:22.840
And we made a small raft
for them that can be for the

00:41:28.650 --> 00:41:32.325
mothers of children
because during the flood

00:41:32.325 --> 00:41:35.640
the lands get very isolated.

00:41:35.640 --> 00:41:39.690
People often cannot get connected,

00:41:39.690 --> 00:41:42.720
or they cannot carry their furniture,

00:41:42.720 --> 00:41:44.940
their household items to a safe place.

00:41:44.940 --> 00:41:46.590
So we thought, okay,

00:41:46.590 --> 00:41:51.590
we went there for a week and
we experimented on a raft,

00:41:53.550 --> 00:41:56.883
how this can be maybe a tiny classroom,

00:41:57.870 --> 00:42:02.013
and it can be used as a
transport during an emergency.

00:42:02.994 --> 00:42:07.994
It was not really a project,

00:42:08.670 --> 00:42:13.170
but it was a project where
we can really get into the

00:42:13.170 --> 00:42:15.903
context, get to know with the people.

00:42:17.220 --> 00:42:18.900
And we made some friends there.

00:42:18.900 --> 00:42:21.422
We found some artisans,
some female artisans.

00:42:21.422 --> 00:42:25.800
They make beautiful weaving mats.

00:42:25.800 --> 00:42:29.352
I have never seen this in
Bangladesh, in anywhere.

00:42:29.352 --> 00:42:31.743
We found those beautiful pieces.

00:42:31.743 --> 00:42:36.720
And this last week maybe,

00:42:36.720 --> 00:42:39.060
we brought those mats to Qatar Summit,

00:42:39.060 --> 00:42:42.450
there is an exhibition and,
they were sold to people,

00:42:42.450 --> 00:42:44.580
and this fund went to the artisans,

00:42:44.580 --> 00:42:46.950
the female artisans
from these rural areas.

00:42:46.950 --> 00:42:49.740
And that's how we're trying
to connect like from different

00:42:49.740 --> 00:42:53.664
rural spaces where they probably
have not seen city much,

00:42:53.664 --> 00:42:55.860
but they are now being connected.

00:42:55.860 --> 00:42:57.874
They're exhibiting their product

00:42:57.874 --> 00:43:00.723
to a exhibition in Dhaka through us.

00:43:02.430 --> 00:43:05.127
We're just trying to be helpful anyhow,

00:43:05.127 --> 00:43:07.230
even through a toilet.

00:43:07.230 --> 00:43:09.480
Like we wanted to design a toilet

00:43:09.480 --> 00:43:10.770
because that was their primary

00:43:10.770 --> 00:43:15.660
need, one of the primary
needs, a nice healthy toilet.

00:43:15.660 --> 00:43:19.885
And we offered them that, we
had some fun and we said, okay,

00:43:19.885 --> 00:43:24.330
don't just waste this on rice or, I mean,

00:43:24.330 --> 00:43:25.388
because they had the food,

00:43:25.388 --> 00:43:28.144
many people were coming with food,

00:43:28.144 --> 00:43:31.800
rice, lentils and everything,
but they didn't want it.

00:43:31.800 --> 00:43:32.950
They didn't ask for it.

00:43:34.080 --> 00:43:34.950
So we thought, okay,

00:43:34.950 --> 00:43:38.008
maybe on an emergency
we can do many things.

00:43:38.008 --> 00:43:42.897
To some families we provided
them with materials,

00:43:42.897 --> 00:43:44.820
what they can repair things with,

00:43:44.820 --> 00:43:49.820
with this sort of toilet where
they can later extend and

00:43:50.220 --> 00:43:53.400
make it a washroom as well.

00:43:53.400 --> 00:43:55.320
So it was always organic.

00:43:55.320 --> 00:43:57.567
It was never one decision
that we are going

00:43:57.567 --> 00:43:59.370
for this and do this, no,

00:43:59.370 --> 00:44:00.643
we always try to go to the context,

00:44:00.643 --> 00:44:02.364
and try to understand, okay,

00:44:02.364 --> 00:44:04.713
this should be the appropriate thing,

00:44:04.713 --> 00:44:06.770
and we try to do that,

00:44:08.220 --> 00:44:10.220
maybe next time we don't build a toilet.

00:44:11.670 --> 00:44:16.670
So yeah, that's for impermanent life.

00:44:17.130 --> 00:44:20.746
I think presence is very
important, because it's always

00:44:20.746 --> 00:44:24.240
changing, it's always new decisions,

00:44:24.240 --> 00:44:26.403
it's always new understanding and

00:44:26.403 --> 00:44:31.403
your presence can make a lot of change.

00:44:31.950 --> 00:44:32.958
That's our observation.

00:44:32.958 --> 00:44:34.674
Whenever you were present there,

00:44:34.674 --> 00:44:38.226
you were still there, and you
try to understand who you were

00:44:38.226 --> 00:44:40.610
working with and what is the purpose.

00:44:40.610 --> 00:44:43.456
If the budget, if the charity,

00:44:43.456 --> 00:44:46.582
if whatever resource we have in hand,

00:44:46.582 --> 00:44:50.450
if we try to figure out that,

00:44:50.450 --> 00:44:52.482
we don't need to do everything,

00:44:52.482 --> 00:44:54.690
we just need to do the
appropriate things sometimes.

00:44:54.690 --> 00:44:58.710
Try to figure out what are
the appropriate things,

00:44:58.710 --> 00:45:03.710
and that's when magic happens,
and that's how we are calling

00:45:04.830 --> 00:45:07.950
it a presence for a wider spectrum.

00:45:07.950 --> 00:45:08.783
Thank you.

00:45:08.783 --> 00:45:11.193
That's all from me.

00:45:12.772 --> 00:45:14.522
I'll stop that slide.

00:45:15.476 --> 00:45:16.467
- I just have a couple
of questions, which are,

00:45:21.613 --> 00:45:26.613
like, in the pictures that you shown,

00:45:26.940 --> 00:45:31.940
there are lots of greenery
and trees around, and in some

00:45:32.940 --> 00:45:37.940
other pictures that I have
seen from the refugee camps,

00:45:39.330 --> 00:45:43.410
Rohingya refugee camps in
Bangladesh, they are very dry.

00:45:43.410 --> 00:45:47.010
Is that the misconception,
that of the picture that I have

00:45:47.010 --> 00:45:49.009
seen before, or did just happened to be

00:45:49.009 --> 00:45:51.134
that where you built this center,

00:45:51.134 --> 00:45:54.624
there were all these
trees that have been there

00:45:54.624 --> 00:45:59.184
for a while because I think
that gives a lot of...

00:45:59.184 --> 00:46:01.874
I mean it's like a transition in between

00:46:01.874 --> 00:46:03.837
the place, it gives a sense

00:46:03.837 --> 00:46:07.763
of peace and tranquility
rather than seeing

00:46:07.763 --> 00:46:11.460
all these dry space around.

00:46:11.460 --> 00:46:16.290
- The Ukhiya-Teknaf area, we
have total internal 32 camps.

00:46:16.290 --> 00:46:18.180
The most photos you see that is in Ukhiya,

00:46:18.180 --> 00:46:22.736
that is the most concentrated zone.

00:46:22.736 --> 00:46:26.683
But we also have a lot of
scattered zone in the periphery,

00:46:26.683 --> 00:46:31.683
which really matches with
the hosting villages.

00:46:33.120 --> 00:46:34.590
And the projects we have,

00:46:34.590 --> 00:46:37.020
I have shown you in first two projects,

00:46:37.020 --> 00:46:41.360
those were in those areas
where the refugee community is

00:46:41.360 --> 00:46:44.460
matching with the host
communities, and those actually

00:46:44.460 --> 00:46:48.180
support both the refugees
and the host nationals.

00:46:48.180 --> 00:46:51.030
So those are very, those are still green.

00:46:51.030 --> 00:46:54.357
But for example, the Rohingya
culture memory center,

00:46:54.357 --> 00:46:59.357
that is exactly in the
middle of the whole camp.

00:47:00.277 --> 00:47:03.993
And you see all the shelters surrounding,

00:47:05.010 --> 00:47:07.447
but the good thing is, when you go now,

00:47:07.447 --> 00:47:10.770
you will see a lot of green,
people have worked on that,

00:47:10.770 --> 00:47:13.650
on projects how to make a greener, cooler,

00:47:13.650 --> 00:47:16.462
and how to revive whatever we had.

00:47:16.462 --> 00:47:19.641
But the first two or three years

00:47:19.641 --> 00:47:21.293
it was very barren, very barren.

00:47:21.293 --> 00:47:23.335
It felt like a desert.

00:47:23.335 --> 00:47:28.335
But now the scenario is a lot better.

00:47:28.560 --> 00:47:33.560
The green is there, it's cool,
the camp is way more livable.

00:47:35.790 --> 00:47:37.200
- Rizvi?

00:47:37.200 --> 00:47:38.056
- [Rizvi] Yes.

00:47:38.056 --> 00:47:39.664
- Thank you so much for that very moving

00:47:39.664 --> 00:47:42.030
and very beautiful talk.

00:47:42.030 --> 00:47:43.792
I mean, the work is so creative,

00:47:43.792 --> 00:47:47.190
and I was struck again how you have

00:47:47.190 --> 00:47:51.180
offered the Rohingya with
whom you have worked,

00:47:51.180 --> 00:47:55.681
a support for heritage and so,

00:47:55.681 --> 00:47:58.944
both heritage and memory.

00:47:58.944 --> 00:48:03.150
And so the architecture
becomes a conduit for this,

00:48:03.150 --> 00:48:06.450
but I realize also it becomes therapeutic.

00:48:06.450 --> 00:48:08.224
So you do address mental health,

00:48:08.224 --> 00:48:11.069
that is one of one of the
very strong things that,

00:48:11.069 --> 00:48:14.016
it comes across that
it becomes therapeutic,

00:48:14.016 --> 00:48:16.860
as I wish you would talk
a little bit more about

00:48:16.860 --> 00:48:18.611
these and the stories that you shared,

00:48:18.611 --> 00:48:22.244
which are so powerful and they are unique.

00:48:22.244 --> 00:48:24.783
You're one of the few architects

00:48:24.783 --> 00:48:26.799
who've worked with genocide.

00:48:26.799 --> 00:48:30.723
So that already puts you
in a unique position.

00:48:31.980 --> 00:48:36.980
- Yes, I mean from our
perspective, what we try to do is,

00:48:39.990 --> 00:48:41.490
at the beginning we thought,

00:48:41.490 --> 00:48:44.610
maybe it's just trauma
that we are dealing with.

00:48:44.610 --> 00:48:46.529
It's just a bad memories.

00:48:46.529 --> 00:48:47.696
And the genocide,

00:48:47.696 --> 00:48:52.696
the genocide was such a
difficult time for them, that we

00:48:53.130 --> 00:48:56.313
overlooked what they had
in past, the good memories.

00:48:57.174 --> 00:49:01.560
And we were, people were lot focusing

00:49:01.560 --> 00:49:04.740
on how you get rid of
that trauma that you had,

00:49:04.740 --> 00:49:07.860
but they were not talking about
what good memories you had,

00:49:07.860 --> 00:49:11.043
what songs you used to sing,
what poetry you used to have.

00:49:12.090 --> 00:49:13.350
So sometimes, I don't know,

00:49:13.350 --> 00:49:15.451
probably memory and
understanding, probably

00:49:15.451 --> 00:49:20.451
we underestimate them
sometimes, that probably it's a

00:49:21.780 --> 00:49:25.890
compartmentalized thing, that
we think they're refugees,

00:49:25.890 --> 00:49:28.072
that's how they need
to be treated in a way.

00:49:28.072 --> 00:49:31.350
But they're also regular
human being as well, right?

00:49:31.350 --> 00:49:35.580
So what they have, they like to have joy,

00:49:35.580 --> 00:49:39.158
they like to dance, they
like to be motivated.

00:49:39.158 --> 00:49:41.328
They don't always like to talk about

00:49:41.328 --> 00:49:42.780
what they're going through.

00:49:42.780 --> 00:49:43.893
No, they don't.

00:49:44.835 --> 00:49:47.850
They often get very distressed.

00:49:47.850 --> 00:49:50.617
I mean whenever a journalist
is coming and asking all these

00:49:50.617 --> 00:49:53.408
difficult questions that stress them out.

00:49:53.408 --> 00:49:54.599
But when you are going,

00:49:54.599 --> 00:49:57.227
sitting with them in a tea stall

00:49:57.227 --> 00:49:58.830
and talking about, you know,
who is your girlfriend?

00:49:58.830 --> 00:50:00.629
Who is your boyfriend?

00:50:00.629 --> 00:50:03.750
That's when they start enjoying, okay,

00:50:03.750 --> 00:50:04.809
we have a life in camp.

00:50:04.809 --> 00:50:08.400
We have not lost everything.

00:50:08.400 --> 00:50:11.130
We have the hope to grow from here.

00:50:11.130 --> 00:50:12.449
So that was our technique.

00:50:12.449 --> 00:50:17.449
We always try to avoid from
our side to talk about the

00:50:18.142 --> 00:50:22.085
genocide or the tough situation,

00:50:22.085 --> 00:50:25.690
try to make the event joyful.

00:50:25.690 --> 00:50:29.640
But there has been a lot of
time where people used to come

00:50:29.640 --> 00:50:34.640
to us and seek for help like
little girls, and guys who were

00:50:35.338 --> 00:50:38.048
in trouble, and in many issues.

00:50:38.048 --> 00:50:39.247
And we used to say, "Okay,

00:50:39.247 --> 00:50:41.670
go to this person, he can help you,

00:50:41.670 --> 00:50:43.200
the government can help you.

00:50:43.200 --> 00:50:44.395
We used to refer them.

00:50:44.395 --> 00:50:49.116
So we were very aware that okay,

00:50:49.116 --> 00:50:53.790
they might come to us with
these problems, that is okay,

00:50:53.790 --> 00:50:56.940
but we don't have to
talk about that always.

00:50:56.940 --> 00:50:59.463
So that's how, we try to enjoy, that's it.

00:51:02.177 --> 00:51:07.177
- So we have a question from Anisha Saha,

00:51:07.488 --> 00:51:11.220
she's from the Department of
Southern Southeast Asia Studies

00:51:11.220 --> 00:51:13.920
at the University of Calcutta.

00:51:13.920 --> 00:51:18.920
And she says that the work
that you, Rizvi are doing is

00:51:20.251 --> 00:51:24.390
absolutely something the
world needs to admire

00:51:24.390 --> 00:51:26.610
and take inspiration from.

00:51:26.610 --> 00:51:30.299
And her question is regarding
the data in relation to

00:51:30.299 --> 00:51:33.681
gender-based violence in the camp,

00:51:33.681 --> 00:51:38.681
that often led to early
pregnancy among teenage girls.

00:51:41.460 --> 00:51:44.220
Is there such a case in Bangladesh?

00:51:44.220 --> 00:51:47.820
And if yes, how is the
organizations you work with

00:51:47.820 --> 00:51:49.980
are thinking with that?

00:51:49.980 --> 00:51:52.627
And the second question
that she has is that,

00:51:52.627 --> 00:51:56.607
when you are in the process of
building this lovely shelter,

00:51:56.607 --> 00:51:59.970
was there any encroachment
issue raised upon the

00:51:59.970 --> 00:52:02.040
organization by the government

00:52:02.040 --> 00:52:04.953
or any other Bangladeshi
regional national?

00:52:06.593 --> 00:52:09.660
- So the first question
is, yes, definitely.

00:52:09.660 --> 00:52:11.310
I mean it happens a lot.

00:52:12.477 --> 00:52:17.477
And in our team we always
try to have energetic female

00:52:17.664 --> 00:52:21.780
members like Kaja Fatme,
who is also an architect.

00:52:21.780 --> 00:52:26.250
She used to work with us in
many projects, and volunteers,

00:52:26.250 --> 00:52:27.540
like female volunteers.

00:52:27.540 --> 00:52:30.870
So these were, because a lot of these

00:52:30.870 --> 00:52:33.000
issues used to arise and they used to

00:52:33.000 --> 00:52:37.298
come with these problems, little
children, adolescent girls.

00:52:37.298 --> 00:52:41.100
And they never used to share this with us.

00:52:41.100 --> 00:52:43.841
Definitely, especially not the guys,

00:52:43.841 --> 00:52:47.550
but they share it with
another female person.

00:52:47.550 --> 00:52:49.350
And that happened a lot of time.

00:52:49.350 --> 00:52:53.910
And there are special teams,
gender bill fellow team,

00:52:53.910 --> 00:52:56.336
who has the counseling service,

00:52:56.336 --> 00:53:00.360
who has the case management services

00:53:00.360 --> 00:53:02.790
and they deal with these issues.

00:53:02.790 --> 00:53:05.755
Any complex issues that arise,

00:53:05.755 --> 00:53:10.755
they try to offer any
health related service.

00:53:11.850 --> 00:53:13.991
They try to offer any
legal related service.

00:53:13.991 --> 00:53:18.660
So BRAC, ActionAid, and
many other organizations,

00:53:18.660 --> 00:53:20.280
they have these services.

00:53:20.280 --> 00:53:22.933
So the center you saw in the beginning

00:53:22.933 --> 00:53:24.638
that that has a courtyard inside.

00:53:24.638 --> 00:53:27.570
One of the room is a counseling room,

00:53:27.570 --> 00:53:30.690
one of the room is a case management room.

00:53:30.690 --> 00:53:33.119
So there is always someone there

00:53:33.119 --> 00:53:36.253
who is there to support these females,

00:53:36.253 --> 00:53:38.631
maybe because they often don't

00:53:38.631 --> 00:53:41.670
share this in front of everyone.

00:53:41.670 --> 00:53:45.971
So we had to make some private
spaces so that they can come

00:53:45.971 --> 00:53:50.216
and feel safe and share
this in a private manner.

00:53:50.216 --> 00:53:54.227
And another thing,
encroachment has not been

00:53:54.227 --> 00:53:58.173
an issue because the
government in the beginning,

00:53:58.173 --> 00:54:03.173
they set in the map in
the plan that this land

00:54:04.990 --> 00:54:07.173
will be for the camp.

00:54:07.173 --> 00:54:09.900
And when we used to build in

00:54:09.900 --> 00:54:11.658
the host community or in a village,

00:54:11.658 --> 00:54:16.050
we used to have this dealing
with the landowner that, okay,

00:54:16.050 --> 00:54:19.437
we are renting this for five
years or six years and they

00:54:19.437 --> 00:54:22.215
give us the land and we build a structure.

00:54:22.215 --> 00:54:24.039
And we have this understanding, okay,

00:54:24.039 --> 00:54:25.824
when we leave, you can use the structure

00:54:25.824 --> 00:54:29.160
or the community can use
the structure, that thing.

00:54:29.160 --> 00:54:31.051
So encroachment was not an issue.

00:54:31.051 --> 00:54:36.051
- You mentioned that the roofing,

00:54:36.537 --> 00:54:40.890
the thatching technique that
is used, goes with the culture

00:54:40.890 --> 00:54:42.525
and I was just wondering
if that is something

00:54:42.525 --> 00:54:46.800
that has been in place, you know,

00:54:46.800 --> 00:54:49.436
forever or is there more of a
story that you could tell us

00:54:49.436 --> 00:54:53.520
about how that technique relates

00:54:53.520 --> 00:54:55.413
or is integrated with the culture?

00:54:58.140 --> 00:54:59.559
- The roofing technique?

00:54:59.559 --> 00:55:03.494
Yes, so the nipa technique,
what we found out is that we

00:55:03.494 --> 00:55:08.494
talked to this craftsman and
they said almost 90% houses

00:55:08.854 --> 00:55:11.700
back in Myanmar, they used to build this

00:55:11.700 --> 00:55:14.130
with this material.

00:55:14.130 --> 00:55:18.703
So about nipa leaf is that
this grows on the salty canals

00:55:18.703 --> 00:55:23.363
only in the salt water
sites in the canals.

00:55:23.363 --> 00:55:28.363
And you cannot find it in
regular water area or something.

00:55:28.710 --> 00:55:33.397
So in Myanmar they grow a lot
of these leaves, and they used

00:55:33.397 --> 00:55:37.499
to have built with this leaf
a lot, and specifically the

00:55:37.499 --> 00:55:40.336
technique that you have seen in the photo.

00:55:40.336 --> 00:55:45.336
They seal the leaves with
the stick of bamboo and

00:55:47.503 --> 00:55:51.720
use it like a tile, you
know, one on another.

00:55:51.720 --> 00:55:54.253
And one day, what happened is that

00:55:54.253 --> 00:55:59.253
we made a sample of this and we are

00:55:59.385 --> 00:56:04.385
taking this by hand with us in the camp.

00:56:05.130 --> 00:56:07.237
And many people started
gathering and they said,

00:56:07.237 --> 00:56:08.250
"Where did you get this?

00:56:08.250 --> 00:56:11.856
This is from our home," and
random people from the camp.

00:56:11.856 --> 00:56:14.010
So that's how it was connecting.

00:56:14.010 --> 00:56:15.540
We're saying, okay,
this is really working.

00:56:15.540 --> 00:56:17.310
And we thought, okay, why not?

00:56:17.310 --> 00:56:18.617
Maybe add some workshops.

00:56:18.617 --> 00:56:23.617
We add this workshop in another
camp in Teknaf to distribute

00:56:23.882 --> 00:56:27.901
the knowledge from the
elderly craftsmen to the young

00:56:27.901 --> 00:56:31.657
who doesn't have any
clue about the technique.

00:56:31.657 --> 00:56:36.657
Even for our country,
this was used before,

00:56:36.840 --> 00:56:38.371
but now they don't use it anymore.

00:56:38.371 --> 00:56:40.590
So it was a learning for us as well.

00:56:40.590 --> 00:56:42.240
We didn't really know about the technique

00:56:42.240 --> 00:56:43.800
we learned from them.

00:56:43.800 --> 00:56:45.180
Now in the Qatar summit,

00:56:45.180 --> 00:56:47.767
we made a wall with nipa palm leaves

00:56:47.767 --> 00:56:49.927
and many people started asking,

00:56:49.927 --> 00:56:51.150
"Okay, is it a coconut leaf?"

00:56:51.150 --> 00:56:52.410
I said, "No, this is a leaf that

00:56:52.410 --> 00:56:55.470
you should know (laughs)
and you should use."

00:56:55.470 --> 00:56:57.423
So that's how it's being connected.

00:56:58.860 --> 00:57:00.642
- Hi, I have a question if that's okay.

00:57:00.642 --> 00:57:04.230
First, thanks so much for
sharing your work with us.

00:57:04.230 --> 00:57:05.493
It's really incredible.

00:57:05.493 --> 00:57:08.403
And I wanted to ask if you
have any advice for students,

00:57:08.403 --> 00:57:11.682
like architecture students
who are interested in working

00:57:11.682 --> 00:57:14.960
with refugees in a similar lens to you.

00:57:14.960 --> 00:57:19.650
Since like I'm from, I'm
Esther's student at Yale,

00:57:19.650 --> 00:57:22.410
and like resources online tend to

00:57:22.410 --> 00:57:25.102
be either very limited
or just limited to formal

00:57:25.102 --> 00:57:28.943
organizations like the UN,
and there's also this added

00:57:28.943 --> 00:57:33.794
concern of it having a
sort of like neocolonialist

00:57:33.794 --> 00:57:38.343
approach for students who are
not like local to the region.

00:57:43.860 --> 00:57:48.860
- I mean, yes, if someone is not local,

00:57:49.020 --> 00:57:50.610
or they don't have much difficulty

00:57:50.610 --> 00:57:53.310
to have these connections,
build connections

00:57:53.310 --> 00:57:55.080
and ability, it's okay.

00:57:55.080 --> 00:57:57.783
I mean you can build a team
or you can get connected with

00:57:57.783 --> 00:58:01.710
the team, with the
locals, with the refugees,

00:58:01.710 --> 00:58:05.520
make friends with them, and
try to connect in a voluntary

00:58:05.520 --> 00:58:10.520
manner or with an organization,
whatever the scope is.

00:58:10.890 --> 00:58:12.687
But it is possible because many,

00:58:12.687 --> 00:58:17.687
many artists or other
professionals from other countries,

00:58:20.250 --> 00:58:22.017
they started working with us.

00:58:22.017 --> 00:58:23.545
They're working here still.

00:58:23.545 --> 00:58:26.291
And what they do is
they team up, they have

00:58:26.291 --> 00:58:31.291
a local person, they have some
volunteers from the refugees.

00:58:31.800 --> 00:58:34.737
So there is a system
where you can blend in.

00:58:34.737 --> 00:58:38.100
When you come to the
context, you are not alone.

00:58:38.100 --> 00:58:40.078
You are not really dealing things alone.

00:58:40.078 --> 00:58:43.740
But the key is what is your intention?

00:58:43.740 --> 00:58:45.220
If you have the intention to help,

00:58:45.220 --> 00:58:49.590
if you really want to come
with everything you have

00:58:49.590 --> 00:58:51.630
and try to be useful,

00:58:51.630 --> 00:58:53.277
people will understand that you are useful

00:58:53.277 --> 00:58:54.727
and they will take your help.

00:58:57.210 --> 00:59:00.105
I see a question from David Palazon.

00:59:00.105 --> 00:59:01.772
David Palazon was...

00:59:03.000 --> 00:59:06.896
David Palazon, if you can come online,

00:59:06.896 --> 00:59:08.130
I'd be very much happy.

00:59:08.130 --> 00:59:11.943
He was the curator of the project.

00:59:13.020 --> 00:59:15.780
He's an artist, he's a filmmaker.

00:59:15.780 --> 00:59:20.780
He has been a huge thing
for this project as well.

00:59:21.300 --> 00:59:25.560
You have seen one photo of
him like working on the,

00:59:25.560 --> 00:59:28.766
you know, workshop, one of the workshops.

00:59:28.766 --> 00:59:33.766
So a lot of ideations,
he has been our teammate,

00:59:34.620 --> 00:59:36.402
our project manager for a long time,

00:59:36.402 --> 00:59:38.520
and we did this together.

00:59:38.520 --> 00:59:40.567
So what was that question, David,

00:59:40.567 --> 00:59:42.930
if you can ask directly at me?

00:59:42.930 --> 00:59:44.610
- Can you hear me, Rizvi?

00:59:44.610 --> 00:59:46.500
- Yes, David, how are you?

00:59:46.500 --> 00:59:47.581
- Long time I see you.

00:59:47.581 --> 00:59:52.581
Congratulations on the
award, and all the award.

00:59:54.330 --> 00:59:57.177
I'm really happy and glad
to see the final result.

00:59:57.177 --> 00:59:58.491
- Thank you so much.

00:59:58.491 --> 01:00:03.491
- And yeah, my question as you know,

01:00:03.720 --> 01:00:07.517
it was quite a complicated
project at RCMC.

01:00:07.517 --> 01:00:11.505
Before you arrive and
after I left. (laughs)

01:00:11.505 --> 01:00:13.745
Let's put it that way.

01:00:13.745 --> 01:00:17.490
There was actually a question
there from somebody who was

01:00:17.490 --> 01:00:22.050
asking whether you had faced
adversity by the authorities

01:00:22.050 --> 01:00:24.123
and camp managers and the government.

01:00:25.260 --> 01:00:30.260
I think that was quite
common, as you could explain,

01:00:32.400 --> 01:00:34.098
or you know, it's not

01:00:34.098 --> 01:00:38.099
as a straightforward as building
anywhere else in the world

01:00:38.099 --> 01:00:41.250
because it's quite a temporary space,

01:00:41.250 --> 01:00:44.010
and we don't know for how
long they're gonna be there.

01:00:44.010 --> 01:00:46.240
And also there's all the situation

01:00:46.240 --> 01:00:51.183
and that has a lot of complexities.

01:00:52.410 --> 01:00:56.460
But my question was more
about the collection.

01:00:56.460 --> 01:00:57.684
So as you know, we sort of conducted

01:00:57.684 --> 01:01:01.112
a massive cultural mapping
with the community,

01:01:01.112 --> 01:01:06.112
which landed in a sort of
production of a lot of objects

01:01:06.210 --> 01:01:09.750
over a thousand objects
and tapestries and so on.

01:01:09.750 --> 01:01:12.804
And although we have different
ideas and there was much

01:01:12.804 --> 01:01:14.847
consultation with the community,

01:01:14.847 --> 01:01:19.378
these defined different areas
as you explain, pottery, and

01:01:19.378 --> 01:01:24.378
architectural models, and
boat making and so on,

01:01:24.630 --> 01:01:26.848
you know, tangible and intangible,

01:01:26.848 --> 01:01:29.190
all the music that we recorded

01:01:29.190 --> 01:01:31.230
in the YouTube channel and so on.

01:01:31.230 --> 01:01:34.655
So all this was like the
material that was supposed to

01:01:34.655 --> 01:01:39.437
influence the space and
the walkthrough experience

01:01:39.437 --> 01:01:44.437
and in the best of the capacity that

01:01:45.124 --> 01:01:49.470
we were able to put this together

01:01:49.470 --> 01:01:52.810
and inform and validate by the community,

01:01:52.810 --> 01:01:56.790
and inform your skills, you know,

01:01:56.790 --> 01:02:00.570
and be glad I found you because
we had a previous architect

01:02:00.570 --> 01:02:02.342
before, which wasn't a very good person.

01:02:02.342 --> 01:02:06.750
So you did an amazing good job.

01:02:06.750 --> 01:02:09.330
So, you know, your engagement

01:02:09.330 --> 01:02:13.077
with the community is a
primer, I have to say.

01:02:13.077 --> 01:02:16.563
So congratulations on that one.

01:02:18.300 --> 01:02:20.820
But yes, you know, to me
it's like the physical,

01:02:20.820 --> 01:02:25.820
you know, the shaping of the culture.

01:02:27.656 --> 01:02:28.906
- I mean, yeah.

01:02:31.008 --> 01:02:32.972
So about the cultural memory center,

01:02:32.972 --> 01:02:37.710
I mean it already took place
with you guys, with you,

01:02:37.710 --> 01:02:41.040
there's Manuel, there
was Dimitrov, there's

01:02:41.040 --> 01:02:43.916
other teammates, Raju, and it was

01:02:43.916 --> 01:02:47.137
already taking some
organic form before, and

01:02:47.137 --> 01:02:51.547
then you guys had us in the team and then

01:02:51.547 --> 01:02:55.290
it shifted to another paradigm.

01:02:55.290 --> 01:03:00.290
So the process of this whole
project was fascinating to me

01:03:01.320 --> 01:03:03.305
because we had to really act on

01:03:03.305 --> 01:03:05.851
what was the demand of the time.

01:03:05.851 --> 01:03:09.055
It was the complexity of the government,

01:03:09.055 --> 01:03:09.888
from the government.

01:03:09.888 --> 01:03:12.120
It was the complexity
within the organization,

01:03:12.120 --> 01:03:14.288
what we can do, what we cannot do.

01:03:14.288 --> 01:03:18.030
And especially from the government,

01:03:18.030 --> 01:03:21.018
because this sort of project
is not easy to get the

01:03:21.018 --> 01:03:23.135
permission from the government, no.

01:03:23.135 --> 01:03:28.135
And we had to wait for the
permission for a long time,

01:03:29.304 --> 01:03:34.110
and I had to change the
drawings, the design,

01:03:34.110 --> 01:03:39.110
simplify it as much as possible,
for like five or six times

01:03:39.150 --> 01:03:40.563
and present it to them.

01:03:42.120 --> 01:03:43.770
So it was always,

01:03:43.770 --> 01:03:46.350
the point we are saying is
presence for a wider spectrum.

01:03:46.350 --> 01:03:49.878
If we had given up at that time, okay,

01:03:49.878 --> 01:03:52.200
the government is not agreeing
with us the first two times

01:03:52.200 --> 01:03:55.650
or three times, then this
project wouldn't happen, right?

01:03:55.650 --> 01:03:59.192
So what we try to do
with whatever we have,

01:03:59.192 --> 01:04:01.560
we have the opportunity

01:04:01.560 --> 01:04:04.483
to do without breaking the harmony,

01:04:04.483 --> 01:04:07.560
without breaking the
sociopolitical scenario,

01:04:07.560 --> 01:04:10.697
without harming anyone
or attacking anyone.

01:04:10.697 --> 01:04:13.950
What is the best possible thing we can do?

01:04:13.950 --> 01:04:16.110
That's how we simplified a lot of things.

01:04:16.110 --> 01:04:18.208
Of course, I remember you had the

01:04:18.208 --> 01:04:21.990
major guidelines were built by you,

01:04:21.990 --> 01:04:24.930
designed by you, that what
should be the inside things,

01:04:24.930 --> 01:04:27.633
and what should the
curation, the narration.

01:04:29.070 --> 01:04:31.320
But then we faced, when you left,

01:04:31.320 --> 01:04:33.547
we faced these issues
from the government that,

01:04:33.547 --> 01:04:35.943
"You cannot do this project in the camp,"

01:04:35.943 --> 01:04:39.997
and campaign chair, he was not convinced,

01:04:39.997 --> 01:04:41.884
"No, this is a cultural project that

01:04:41.884 --> 01:04:45.540
is not applicable in the camp."

01:04:45.540 --> 01:04:46.620
So what we try to do,

01:04:46.620 --> 01:04:49.202
we try to shift it a
lot to learning things

01:04:49.202 --> 01:04:51.750
like learning space, training space.

01:04:51.750 --> 01:04:54.630
And we had to convince them that

01:04:54.630 --> 01:04:56.400
this is a mental health facility.

01:04:56.400 --> 01:04:58.530
This is something that is
related to mental health.

01:04:58.530 --> 01:04:59.910
Their culture is there,

01:04:59.910 --> 01:05:02.760
but it's not luxury thing,

01:05:02.760 --> 01:05:05.520
or it's not something that you can

01:05:05.520 --> 01:05:07.649
have in Dhaka or other
cities or other communities.

01:05:07.649 --> 01:05:12.649
What can be the translated
version for the refugees?

01:05:12.840 --> 01:05:14.730
What can be the simplified way,

01:05:14.730 --> 01:05:19.730
to not really be a threat to
also the hosting nationals.

01:05:21.690 --> 01:05:25.296
We had to take that in mind,
and we simplified a lot.

01:05:25.296 --> 01:05:27.817
The facades were like organics.

01:05:27.817 --> 01:05:30.363
I don't know if you remember,
there was a large board on

01:05:30.363 --> 01:05:35.246
the outside of the, we had
to erase all of this, right?

01:05:35.246 --> 01:05:39.676
And even during the
construction, it was really,

01:05:39.676 --> 01:05:43.091
I'll tell you, during
the construction period,

01:05:43.091 --> 01:05:46.650
we got the support from the artisans,

01:05:46.650 --> 01:05:49.188
but the time was short.

01:05:49.188 --> 01:05:53.610
We already lost a lot of time
for the Covid period. (laughs)

01:05:53.610 --> 01:05:56.100
And during this time, I don't know,

01:05:56.100 --> 01:05:58.743
this was the best thing
we could come up with.

01:05:58.743 --> 01:06:02.190
And, and I said it's the memory

01:06:02.190 --> 01:06:04.733
that we will carry,
during the making of the

01:06:04.733 --> 01:06:09.711
tiles, the partitions,
and even the curators,

01:06:09.711 --> 01:06:11.820
the present curators.

01:06:11.820 --> 01:06:15.210
They're working on the new
products, the new things.

01:06:15.210 --> 01:06:17.760
And I'm sure they are
just working on growing

01:06:17.760 --> 01:06:20.160
the whole thing more and more.

01:06:20.160 --> 01:06:22.659
I'm not in touch with
the IOM team anymore,

01:06:22.659 --> 01:06:26.700
but I have the
communication with artisans,

01:06:26.700 --> 01:06:29.910
and I often see photos what's going on,

01:06:29.910 --> 01:06:32.100
and they're gradually growing.

01:06:32.100 --> 01:06:36.039
They're making small villages
or whatever we had in in mind.

01:06:36.039 --> 01:06:38.320
I think it'll not stop.

01:06:38.320 --> 01:06:42.439
It's not a fixed thing
that we're looking at it,

01:06:42.439 --> 01:06:44.364
like if they stay here in five years,

01:06:44.364 --> 01:06:48.809
probably you'll see something
more crazy growing there.

01:06:48.809 --> 01:06:50.250
- No, for sure.

01:06:50.250 --> 01:06:51.083
I mean, you know,

01:06:51.083 --> 01:06:53.019
the guidelines were set and the map

01:06:53.019 --> 01:06:56.032
is there to be explore and expanded.

01:06:56.032 --> 01:06:59.081
But to me the soul of the building

01:06:59.081 --> 01:07:03.690
was all these objects, you know?

01:07:03.690 --> 01:07:05.910
I remember before there
was a building, you know,

01:07:05.910 --> 01:07:07.290
because to me it was, you know,

01:07:07.290 --> 01:07:11.250
I was hire as a curator of
a center that didn't exist,

01:07:11.250 --> 01:07:13.530
of a collection that didn't exist.

01:07:13.530 --> 01:07:15.900
So I have to create, you know,

01:07:15.900 --> 01:07:19.002
the team that will create
with the community,

01:07:19.002 --> 01:07:22.976
a collection that will tell
a narrative and you know,

01:07:22.976 --> 01:07:25.590
and you have to make a building

01:07:25.590 --> 01:07:27.510
to shape that narrative, you know?

01:07:27.510 --> 01:07:30.310
So it was quite an
impossible project. (laughs)

01:07:30.310 --> 01:07:33.775
'Cause the UN don't do cultural centers.

01:07:33.775 --> 01:07:34.950
- No, they don't. (laughs)

01:07:34.950 --> 01:07:36.567
- Anywhere in the world.

01:07:36.567 --> 01:07:38.715
The least in a refugee camp. (laughs)

01:07:38.715 --> 01:07:41.645
So, you know, people have,

01:07:41.645 --> 01:07:44.999
what we went through to make...

01:07:44.999 --> 01:07:48.430
And Manuel, you know, Manuel is, you know,

01:07:48.430 --> 01:07:51.540
also he have to deal with many things.

01:07:51.540 --> 01:07:52.373
We all did.

01:07:54.068 --> 01:07:56.340
But I'm glad, you know,

01:07:56.340 --> 01:07:59.190
is finally there and
in some sort of shape,

01:07:59.190 --> 01:08:02.940
and content is evolving
into something that

01:08:02.940 --> 01:08:06.570
the community can hold on onto in,

01:08:06.570 --> 01:08:09.570
in the adversity of what they're facing,

01:08:09.570 --> 01:08:13.724
which is unclear how
long, (chuckles) you know,

01:08:13.724 --> 01:08:15.912
they have to be there, you know?

01:08:15.912 --> 01:08:16.745
For sure.

01:08:16.745 --> 01:08:21.150
It is framing to the MHPSS
sort of framework, because

01:08:21.150 --> 01:08:23.640
that's how it is justifying the UN system.

01:08:23.640 --> 01:08:26.764
But, it is very cultural.

01:08:26.764 --> 01:08:30.373
- Exactly, but what's the
most beautiful thing is,

01:08:30.373 --> 01:08:35.373
what you shared with
artisans, the trainings,

01:08:35.892 --> 01:08:38.818
the house model making.

01:08:38.818 --> 01:08:41.213
I mean, even if these products are gone,

01:08:41.213 --> 01:08:45.540
these people are the living
legions of the community now.

01:08:45.540 --> 01:08:46.782
I mean, you cannot deny that

01:08:46.782 --> 01:08:49.770
even if the whole facility is not there.

01:08:49.770 --> 01:08:54.000
This team, this massive
team is there to share,

01:08:54.000 --> 01:08:55.920
to do all the right
things for the community.

01:08:55.920 --> 01:08:58.020
And that's a huge success of you,

01:08:58.020 --> 01:08:59.998
of the team, of us together.

01:08:59.998 --> 01:09:02.430
- Yeah, they're growing too,

01:09:02.430 --> 01:09:05.100
they're curating their
own life and culture.

01:09:05.100 --> 01:09:08.313
They didn't know, you
see, we are the catalyst

01:09:08.313 --> 01:09:13.313
that provide them with the
opportunity to be their own

01:09:13.950 --> 01:09:17.250
curators and to be the culture men,

01:09:17.250 --> 01:09:22.250
and sort of lead that cultural revolution.

01:09:22.452 --> 01:09:24.769
Anyway, I will not stop.

01:09:24.769 --> 01:09:29.040
Maybe I should let you keep going.

01:09:29.040 --> 01:09:30.180
Thank you very much.

01:09:30.180 --> 01:09:32.320
- Yeah, talk to you later, David.

01:09:33.328 --> 01:09:35.480
- Hi, I actually have a question as well.

01:09:35.480 --> 01:09:37.890
I already dropped my question in chat,

01:09:37.890 --> 01:09:39.870
but I thought it'd be nice to ask it here.

01:09:39.870 --> 01:09:42.162
I'm just wondering if you can
speak a little bit more about

01:09:42.162 --> 01:09:46.050
the role of peace-building
humanitarian organizations,

01:09:46.050 --> 01:09:50.490
nonprofits, and international aid agencies

01:09:50.490 --> 01:09:53.468
in making the project that you do happen.

01:09:53.468 --> 01:09:55.410
Since you mentioned the IOM,

01:09:55.410 --> 01:09:57.690
you mentioned BRAC and a
few other organizations

01:09:57.690 --> 01:10:01.560
I'm just wondering whether
their role in making your

01:10:01.560 --> 01:10:03.816
projects happen are mostly
on the, you know, funding,

01:10:03.816 --> 01:10:07.890
the program design side in
terms of the overall community

01:10:07.890 --> 01:10:11.638
development programs, or they're
also providing, you know,

01:10:11.638 --> 01:10:15.287
access to communities, and
also helping you lead with the

01:10:15.287 --> 01:10:17.121
government to make sure that,

01:10:17.121 --> 01:10:21.076
to kind of meet their vision
and their requirements.

01:10:21.076 --> 01:10:23.010
Yeah, just speak a little bit about that.

01:10:23.010 --> 01:10:24.330
Thank you.

01:10:24.330 --> 01:10:29.130
- Yeah, I mean had this confusion

01:10:29.130 --> 01:10:31.680
that whether we can explore to the

01:10:31.680 --> 01:10:35.370
strict systems of UN
or other organizations,

01:10:35.370 --> 01:10:40.370
and do these unconventional things,

01:10:41.550 --> 01:10:46.110
but we had to take the
coverage to do that.

01:10:46.110 --> 01:10:47.791
I mean, the funding was there,

01:10:47.791 --> 01:10:49.069
the projects and you know,

01:10:49.069 --> 01:10:54.069
the project has some sort of guidelines,

01:10:54.720 --> 01:10:56.940
but when you are there as a catalyst,

01:10:56.940 --> 01:10:57.773
and you say this is

01:10:57.773 --> 01:10:59.520
the right thing to do under this project,

01:10:59.520 --> 01:11:02.550
there is always chance to improvise.

01:11:02.550 --> 01:11:04.044
There is always chance to

01:11:04.044 --> 01:11:08.457
shift it a bit in the right direction,

01:11:08.457 --> 01:11:10.424
and come up with a nice,

01:11:10.424 --> 01:11:14.439
I mean, very appropriate
thing I would say.

01:11:14.439 --> 01:11:17.430
We often think, okay,

01:11:17.430 --> 01:11:19.410
these are the projects
I have to just do this.

01:11:19.410 --> 01:11:23.782
That is the biggest
criticism of NGOs probably,

01:11:23.782 --> 01:11:27.210
that they always operate from outside.

01:11:27.210 --> 01:11:29.940
They bring the funding, they
have to just do this, this,

01:11:29.940 --> 01:11:31.364
this, fill up the checklist.

01:11:31.364 --> 01:11:34.293
And sometimes it's a mess instead,

01:11:36.090 --> 01:11:38.610
but in many cases it's not a mess.

01:11:38.610 --> 01:11:41.190
It's actually very helpful
and very beautiful,

01:11:41.190 --> 01:11:46.110
and that's all because of
the person who is there,

01:11:46.110 --> 01:11:47.280
who is operating.

01:11:47.280 --> 01:11:48.630
I mean, in the organizations,

01:11:48.630 --> 01:11:50.460
I have met so many brave persons,

01:11:50.460 --> 01:11:54.296
so many visionary person,
who actually could think in a

01:11:54.296 --> 01:11:56.780
creative way, in a visionary way,

01:11:56.780 --> 01:12:00.660
and they could improvise
whatever they need.

01:12:00.660 --> 01:12:02.596
They could even come up with new projects

01:12:02.596 --> 01:12:05.252
like the RCMC, the Rohingya
Culture Memory Center.

01:12:05.252 --> 01:12:08.880
The team worked to build
this project, right?

01:12:08.880 --> 01:12:12.568
They created this project
under mental health.

01:12:12.568 --> 01:12:17.568
So there is always chance for
the people who are in charge

01:12:17.870 --> 01:12:21.210
to really have this visionary decisions.

01:12:21.210 --> 01:12:24.050
And if you can really encourage them,

01:12:24.050 --> 01:12:26.910
come up with a nice idea, and slowly,

01:12:26.910 --> 01:12:28.350
you don't have to always push them.

01:12:28.350 --> 01:12:30.930
Just slowly have discussions.

01:12:30.930 --> 01:12:32.820
And there are a lot of people,

01:12:32.820 --> 01:12:35.137
a lot of positive people
to really make change.

01:12:35.137 --> 01:12:38.730
There are a lot of people
who don't want to do much.

01:12:38.730 --> 01:12:41.430
They want to just (chuckles)
do their desk job,

01:12:41.430 --> 01:12:44.850
but a lot of people are actually
making change and they're

01:12:44.850 --> 01:12:48.753
actually do this benefits to the people.

01:12:50.640 --> 01:12:55.640
- So I was just asked to thank
you for this extraordinary,

01:12:56.550 --> 01:12:59.040
really deeply moving presentation.

01:12:59.040 --> 01:13:02.940
We were honored to hear you,
and we look forward to having

01:13:02.940 --> 01:13:04.609
this posted on our website,

01:13:04.609 --> 01:13:08.063
and look forward to continuing
to follow your career

01:13:08.063 --> 01:13:12.122
and congratulate and
salute you for everything

01:13:12.122 --> 01:13:14.070
you have done for the Rohingyas,

01:13:14.070 --> 01:13:18.020
and the path you have
shown to the profession.

01:13:18.020 --> 01:13:20.130
That is no mean feat.

01:13:20.130 --> 01:13:21.750
It's an extraordinary achievement.

01:13:21.750 --> 01:13:24.097
So thank you. Thank you so much.

01:13:24.097 --> 01:13:26.339
Thank you very, very much.

01:13:26.339 --> 01:13:27.681
- [Rizvi] Thank you so much.

01:13:27.681 --> 01:13:28.839
- And thanks to all of
you for being present

01:13:28.839 --> 01:13:30.902
at this event.

01:13:30.902 --> 01:13:34.134
We will certainly be
following Rizvi Hassan

01:13:34.134 --> 01:13:39.134
as he goes along at his career, thank you.

01:13:39.280 --> 01:13:40.817
- Thank you so much for having us,

01:13:40.817 --> 01:13:43.069
and I really enjoyed the time.

01:13:43.069 --> 01:13:44.986
Hope to see you again.

